BEGIN;

-- right-of-use-assets-net-lessee-accounting
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Since PSAK 116 a lessee puts almost every lease on its balance sheet as a right-of-use asset and a lease liability. The liability is the present value of the lease payments over the lease term, and the asset is the liability plus prepayments, initial direct costs and restoration costs, less incentives received.</p></li><li><p>Two judgments drive the numbers. One is whether the contract is a lease at all, which turns on who controls an identified asset. The other is the lease term, which includes option periods the lessee is reasonably certain to use.</p></li><li><p>Afterwards the asset is depreciated, usually straight-line, while interest on the liability falls as it is repaid, so a lease costs more in its early years than the even rent of the old standard. Changes in term, index-linked rent or scope remeasure the liability and move the asset with it.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report right-of-use assets, net, of 123,977 at 31 December 2024 against 203,379 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11). The line fell 39.0 percent and is 0.4 percent of total assets (0.7 percent a year earlier). Against it stand lease liabilities of 112,926, of which 50,575 is current and 62,351 non-current, against 173,189 a year earlier (87,223 and 85,966) (Note 30, Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163). This page covers both liability lines.</p><p>A student should come away able to decide whether a contract holds a lease, measure the asset and liability, run them through their life with journals, and read a lessee's lease notes.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 116 <em>Sewa</em> (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16), which PHE adopted on 1 January 2020 (Note 18, Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129).</p><h2>1. Why leases are on the lessee's balance sheet</h2><p>Under the old standard, PSAK 30 (IAS 17), a lessee split leases in two. A <em>finance lease</em>, one that transferred substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership, went on the balance sheet. An <em>operating lease</em> did not. The lessee expensed the rent and disclosed its commitments in a note. Kieso calls the result off-balance-sheet financing. Two airlines flying identical fleets could show very different debt depending on how their leases were drafted.</p><p>PSAK 116 removes the split for lessees. A lease gives the lessee a right to use an asset, which is an asset, and an obligation to pay for it, which is a liability, whatever the contract is called. For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary adds to its ships and aircraft class (45,240 net at the end of 2024, Note 18) by chartering a supply vessel for three years at 1,000 a year, paid at each year end, and could borrow for three years at 6 percent.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 30, operating lease</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 116</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance sheet at the start</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>nothing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>asset and liability of 1,000 × 2.673012 = 2,673.01</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit or loss each year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>rent expense 1,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>depreciation plus interest</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>2.673012</em> is the annuity factor at 6 percent for three years, (1 − 1.06^−3) / 0.06, the present value of 1 a year.</p><h2>2. Is the contract a lease?</h2><p>A contract is or contains a lease if it conveys the right to control the use of an <em>identified asset</em> for a period in exchange for consideration (para. 9). PHE's policy uses the same words (Note 3n, Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74). Control has two parts, tested over the period of use.</p><ul><li><p><em>Identified asset</em>. The asset is named or implied in the contract, and the supplier has no <em>substantive</em> right to substitute another. A substitution right is substantive only if the supplier can practically swap the asset and would benefit from doing so. A right to substitute only when the asset breaks down does not count.</p></li><li><p><em>Right to control</em>. The customer obtains substantially all the economic benefits from use, and directs how and for what purpose the asset is used.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), the charter names one vessel. The subsidiary decides which platforms it serves, in what order and when it sails, while the owner's crew operates it. The customer directs the use, so the charter contains a lease. If instead a marine contractor agreed to move a set tonnage of cargo each month with whichever boats it chose, the contractor would be directing its own fleet and the subsidiary would be buying a transport service, with nothing on this line.</p><p><strong>Separating components.</strong> A charter usually pays for the vessel and for services such as crew, fuel management and catering. The consideration is split on relative stand-alone prices, and only the lease component goes into the liability (paras. 12–13). Suppose the day rate is 30, a bareboat charter (the vessel alone, with the charterer finding the crew) would cost 21 a day, and comparable crew services would cost 14.</p><blockquote><p>Lease component = 30 × 21 / (21 + 14) = 18 a day; annual lease payment = 18 × 365 = 6,570</p><p>Non-lease component = 30 × 14 / 35 = 12 a day; 12 × 365 = 4,380 a year, expensed as the services are received</p></blockquote><p>A lessee may elect, by class of asset, to treat the whole payment as lease (para. 15), which would put 30 × 365 = 10,950 a year into the liability instead of 6,570.</p><h2>3. The lease term</h2><p>The <em>lease term</em> is the non-cancellable period plus periods under an extension option the lessee is <em>reasonably certain</em> to exercise, and periods under a termination option it is reasonably certain not to exercise (para. 18). Reasonably certain is a high threshold, above "more likely than not". The entity weighs every economic incentive, such as rent in the option period against market rent, significant improvements it has made to the asset, the cost of relocating, and how important the asset is to its operations (para. 19).</p><p>For example (illustration), the vessel charter runs three years with an option to extend for two more at the same rate. At commencement the subsidiary's field work is contracted for only three years, so the extension is not reasonably certain and the term is three years. The term is reassessed only when a significant event within the lessee's control changes that view (para. 20), which section 8 picks up.</p><h2>4. Initial measurement</h2><p><strong>The lease liability</strong> is the present value of the lease payments not yet paid at commencement (para. 26). <em>Lease payments</em> are (para. 27):</p><ul><li><p>fixed payments, including payments that are fixed in substance, less incentives receivable;</p></li><li><p>variable payments that depend on an index or a rate, measured at the index or rate at commencement;</p></li><li><p>amounts the lessee expects to pay under a <em>residual value guarantee</em>, a promise that the asset will be worth at least a set amount at the end;</p></li><li><p>the exercise price of a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise;</p></li><li><p>penalties for terminating the lease, if the term assumes termination.</p></li></ul><p>The discount rate is the <em>rate implicit in the lease</em> if it can be readily determined, otherwise the lessee's <em>incremental borrowing rate</em>, what it would pay to borrow over a similar term with similar security for an asset of similar value. PHE uses the incremental borrowing rate, because the implicit rate is not readily determinable (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76).</p><p><strong>The right-of-use asset</strong> is the liability plus lease payments made at or before commencement, initial direct costs, and the estimated cost of dismantling or restoring the asset that the lease obliges the lessee to bear, less lease incentives received (para. 24). PHE's policy lists the same components except restoration costs (Note 3n, Daftar 5/58, PDF p. 75).</p><p>For example (illustration), the vessel charter pays 6,570 a year in arrears for three years at an incremental borrowing rate of 6 percent. The subsidiary pays a broker 120 to arrange it, and the owner pays the subsidiary 150 toward fitting the vessel with its equipment.</p><blockquote><p>Lease liability = 6,570 × 2.673012 = 17,561.69</p><p>Right-of-use asset = 17,561.69 + 120 initial direct costs − 150 incentive = 17,531.69</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Account</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Debit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Credit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,561.69</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Lease liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,561.69</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets (broker's fee)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>120.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>120.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash (incentive received)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>150.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>150.00</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>5. Subsequent measurement</h2><p>The two sides now follow different rules. The liability is carried at amortised cost, with interest added at the discount rate on the opening balance and payments deducted (the <em>effective interest method</em>). The asset is depreciated under PSAK 216's rules, usually straight-line (PHE's policy), from commencement over the shorter of the lease term and its useful life, or over its useful life if ownership passes or a purchase option is reasonably certain (paras. 31–32). It is also tested for impairment under PSAK 236 (para. 33).</p><table style="min-width: 175px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening liability</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest at 6%</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Payment</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing liability</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Depreciation</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest + depreciation</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,561.69</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,053.70</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,570.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,045.39</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,843.90</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,897.60</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,045.39</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>722.72</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,570.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,198.11</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,843.90</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,566.62</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,198.11</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>371.89</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,570.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,843.89</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,215.78</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Depreciation = 17,531.69 / 3 = 5,843.90 a year, with 0.01 of rounding in year 3</p><p>Total charge = 1,053.70 + 722.72 + 371.89 + 17,531.69 = 19,680.00 = 3 × 6,570 + 120 − 150</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year-1 journals</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 1,053.70, Dr Lease liabilities 5,516.30 / Cr Cash 6,570.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>payment</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Depreciation expense 5,843.90 / Cr Accumulated depreciation, right-of-use assets 5,843.90</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>depreciation</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The total over three years is the same as under PSAK 30, where the cost would have been (19,710 + 120 − 150) / 3 = 6,560 a year. The timing differs. PSAK 116 charges 337.60 more in year 1 and 344.22 less in year 3, because interest is largest when the liability is largest. A portfolio of leases of different ages smooths this out, but a single new lease is front-loaded.</p><p>The statements also look different. Depreciation sits in operating expenses and interest in finance costs, so EBITDA (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation) rises when a company moves from PSAK 30 to PSAK 116. In the cash flow statement the principal part of each payment is a financing outflow, and payments for short-term and low-value leases and variable payments outside the liability are operating (para. 50). PHE shows "payments of lease liabilities" of 146,158 in financing activities (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17).</p><h2>6. Variable payments, guarantees and purchase options</h2><p>The standard sorts variable payments by what they depend on.</p><p><strong>Index- or rate-linked payments</strong> go into the liability at the current index, and when the index moves the liability is remeasured at the original discount rate (here 6 percent), with the asset adjusted by the same amount (paras. 39, 42–43). For example (illustration), an office lease pays 500 a year for five years, linked to the consumer price index. After the second payment the index rises 4 percent, so the three remaining payments become 520.</p><blockquote><p>Increase in lease liability = 20 × 2.673012 = 53.46</p><p>Dr Right-of-use assets 53.46 / Cr Lease liabilities 53.46</p></blockquote><p><strong>Usage- or performance-based payments</strong>, such as a stand-by rate per day a rig waits on weather, or rent as a share of a shop's sales, stay out of the liability and are expensed when they arise (para. 38). PHE's policy says the same (Note 3n, PDF p. 75).</p><p><strong>Residual value guarantees</strong> enter the liability only at the amount the lessee expects to pay under them.</p><p><strong>Purchase options</strong> reasonably certain to be exercised enter the liability at their exercise price, and the asset is then depreciated over its useful life because the lessee expects to keep it. For example (illustration), a subsidiary leases a vehicle for three years at 30 a year with an option to buy it for 10 at the end, which it is reasonably certain to take. The vehicle will last six years and the incremental borrowing rate is 7 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Lease liability = 30 × 2.624316 + 10 / 1.07^3 = 78.73 + 8.16 = 86.89</p><p>Depreciation = 86.89 / 6 = 14.48 a year, over the useful life</p></blockquote><h2>7. Short-term and low-value leases</h2><p>Two exemptions let a lessee skip the balance sheet and expense payments straight-line (paras. 5–8). A <em>short-term lease</em> has a term of 12 months or less at commencement and no purchase option, and the election is made by class of asset. A <em>low-value lease</em> is judged lease by lease on the value of the asset when new; the IASB had in mind items such as laptops and phones, around USD 5,000 or less in whole dollars. PHE applies both (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76). For example (illustration), a four-month crane hire at 25 a month.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Lease expense, short-term 25 / Cr Cash 25, each month; 100 in total</p></blockquote><p>A short-term lease that is extended or modified becomes a new lease and is tested again (para. 7).</p><h2>8. Reassessments and modifications</h2><p><strong>Reassessment</strong> happens when something already in the contract is seen differently, for example when the lessee becomes reasonably certain to use an extension option, or stops being certain. The liability is remeasured with the revised payments at a revised discount rate, the current incremental borrowing rate, and the asset moves by the same amount (paras. 39–41). For example (illustration), at the end of year 1 the subsidiary signs a five-year field contract that needs the vessel, an event within its control that makes the two-year extension reasonably certain. Four payments now remain, and its incremental borrowing rate has risen to 7 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Revised liability = 6,570 × 3.387211 = 22,253.98, where 3.387211 = (1 − 1.07^−4) / 0.07</p><p>Increase = 22,253.98 − 12,045.39 = 10,208.59</p><p>Right-of-use asset = (17,531.69 − 5,843.90) + 10,208.59 = 21,896.38, depreciated over four years at 5,474.10 a year</p><p>Dr Right-of-use assets 10,208.59 / Cr Lease liabilities 10,208.59</p></blockquote><p><strong>A modification</strong> is a change in scope or price outside the original terms (para. 44). If it adds the right to use another asset at a price matching its stand-alone price, it is a separate new lease. If it reduces scope, for example by returning a physically distinct asset, the lessee first removes the proportion of asset and liability given up, with the difference in profit or loss, and then remeasures what remains at a revised rate (para. 46(a)). Half of one vessel's capacity would not do, because a capacity portion that is not physically distinct is not an identified asset (para. B20). For example (illustration), suppose the charter had covered two identical vessels, each carrying half the payments, and at the end of year 1 the owner agreed to take one back for the remaining two years. Half of the asset (11,687.79 × 50% = 5,843.90) and half of the liability (12,045.39 × 50% = 6,022.70) are derecognised.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Lease liabilities 6,022.70 / Cr Right-of-use assets 5,843.90 / Cr Gain on lease modification 178.80</p></blockquote><p>The remaining liability would then be remeasured on the new payments, with the asset adjusted. PHE's policy lists modifications, changes in term or payments and changes in purchase-option assessments as triggers for remeasurement (Note 3n, PDF p. 76).</p><h2>9. Impairment, other models and presentation</h2><p>A right-of-use asset is tested for impairment like any fixed asset, usually within a cash-generating unit (para. 33). If the lessee revalues the class of fixed assets the leased asset belongs to, it may revalue the right-of-use assets in that class too (para. 35). A right-of-use asset that meets the definition of investment property, a building leased and sublet for rent, follows PSAK 240 (para. 34).</p><p>Right-of-use assets and lease liabilities are presented separately or disclosed by line, and interest on the liability is presented as a finance cost (paras. 47, 49). Disclosure covers depreciation by class, interest, short-term and low-value expense, variable payments, total cash outflow, additions, and carrying amounts by class (para. 53), plus a maturity analysis of the liability.</p><p>The liability is split between current and non-current under PSAK 201 like any borrowing. The current portion is the principal to be repaid within twelve months, not the payments due. For example (illustration), the vessel liability of 12,045.39 at the end of year 1 splits as follows.</p><blockquote><p>Current = 6,570.00 − 722.72 interest of year 2 = 5,847.28</p><p>Non-current = 12,045.39 − 5,847.28 = 6,198.11, the closing liability of year 2</p></blockquote><p>The maturity analysis shows undiscounted payments (para. 58), so its first bucket, 6,570, exceeds the current portion by the interest still to accrue.</p><h2>10. Sale and leaseback</h2><p>In a <em>sale and leaseback</em> the owner sells an asset and leases it straight back. If the transfer is a sale under PSAK 115, the seller keeps on its books only the part of the old carrying amount that relates to the right of use it retains, and recognises a gain only on the rights actually passed to the buyer (paras. 99–100). If the transfer is not a sale, the asset stays and the cash is a financial liability (para. 103). A 2022 amendment, which PHE notes had no significant effect on it, stops the seller-lessee from measuring the lease liability in a way that recognises gain on the retained right (Note 2, Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary sells an office building with a carrying amount of 600 to an investor for 1,000, its fair value, and leases it back for five years at 80 a year in arrears. The incremental borrowing rate is 6 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Lease liability = 80 × 4.212364 = 336.99</p><p>Right-of-use asset = 600 × 336.99 / 1,000 = 202.19, the share of the old carrying amount retained</p><p>Total gain = 1,000 − 600 = 400; gain recognised = 400 × (1,000 − 336.99) / 1,000 = 265.20</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Account</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Debit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Credit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,000.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>202.19</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Building (carrying amount)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>600.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Lease liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>336.99</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gain on sale and leaseback</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>265.20</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The remaining 134.80 of the gain is never booked as such. It is embedded in the lower right-of-use asset and reaches profit through lower depreciation over the five years.</p><h2>11. The lessor's side, briefly</h2><p>Lessors keep the two-model split that lessees lost. A lease that transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership is a finance lease, and the lessor replaces the asset with a receivable. Any other lease is an operating lease, and the lessor keeps the asset and recognises rent straight-line (paras. 61–66). Kieso's indicators are transfer of ownership, a purchase option priced to be exercised, a term covering the major part of the asset's life, and payments with a present value close to its fair value. PHE's lessor policy describes operating leases, with rent recognised straight-line in other operating income (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76).</p><h2>12. Right-of-use assets across industries</h2><p>PSAK 116 matters most where the business runs on assets it does not own.</p><p><strong>Airlines.</strong> Aircraft are the classic leased asset, and many Indonesian carriers lease most of their fleets. Leases often carry <em>return conditions</em>, obligations to hand the aircraft back with a set amount of engine and airframe life, which add restoration-type costs to the asset as the obligation arises, with a provision under PSAK 237, and maintenance reserves paid to the lessor during the term.</p><p><strong>Telecommunications and towers.</strong> Mobile operators lease space on towers, rooftops and land for their sites, often for long terms with renewal options, so the lease term judgment in section 3 drives the liability. The tower companies on the other side are lessors with operating leases.</p><p><strong>Retail and banking.</strong> Shops and bank branches are leased buildings. Rent that varies with sales stays out of the liability under section 6, so a retailer on turnover rent carries a smaller liability than one on fixed rent for the same store.</p><p><strong>Shipping and offshore services.</strong> A <em>bareboat charter</em> (vessel only) is usually a lease. A <em>time charter</em> (vessel with crew, the charterer directing where it goes) usually contains one, with the crew as a non-lease component, as in section 2. A <em>voyage charter</em>, where the owner directs the vessel to deliver a cargo, is usually a service.</p><p><strong>Land rights.</strong> Indonesian land rights such as <em>hak guna bangunan</em> are tested under ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36). A right that is in substance a land purchase is a fixed asset, and a right to use is a lease. PHE applies that test (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69) and carries land rights of 127, net, in this line.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> An <em>ijarah</em>, the sharia lease, is accounted for under PSAK 407 <em>Akuntansi Ijarah</em> (formerly PSAK 107) where the entity applies the sharia standards (STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED).</p><p><strong>Upstream oil and gas.</strong> Leases to explore for or use oil and gas are outside PSAK 116 (para. 3(a)), which covers the right to the resource itself. Rigs, vessels, tanks and buildings used in those operations are inside it. When an operator signs a lease for a joint operation, it recognises the full liability if it is the party the lessor can pursue for payment, and recovers the partners' shares through joint billings (IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision, March 2019).</p><h2>13. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 18 and 30</h2><p>PHE's Note 18 shows cost and accumulated depreciation by class (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). Net book values, computed from that table:</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Class</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Land rights</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>127</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>176</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Buildings</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>14,043</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>22,368</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Tanks, pipeline installations and other equipment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>48,839</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>54,960</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Ships and aircraft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>45,240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>56,207</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Movable assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,728</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>69,668</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Right-of-use assets, net</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>123,977</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>203,379</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>203,379 + additions 22,519 − cost "correction" 131,884 − reclassification 450 − depreciation 107,172 + accumulated depreciation "correction" 136,834 + reclassification 751 = 123,977</p></blockquote><p>The correction columns change cost by −131,884 and accumulated depreciation by +136,834, raising net book value by 4,950. They do not offset class by class, and PHE does not explain them. Two ratios are consistent with a portfolio running off.</p><blockquote><p>Accumulated depreciation / cost = 901,080 / 1,025,057 = 87.9% (2023: 931,493 / 1,134,872 = 82.1%)</p><p>Undiscounted payments due within one year = 61,542 / 119,135 = 51.7%; due after five years = 393</p></blockquote><p>The liability side reconciles in Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163).</p><blockquote><p>173,189 + additions 82,334 + interest 7,354 − payments 146,158 − exchange differences 3,793 = 112,926</p><p>Implied interest rate = 7,354 / ((173,189 + 112,926) / 2) = 5.1%</p><p>Undiscounted payments 119,135 − future interest 6,209 = 112,926 = current 50,575 + non-current 62,351</p></blockquote><p>The split does not fit section 9 on the printed figures. Payments due within one year are 61,542, so even if all 6,209 of future interest fell in that year, principal due within it would be at least 61,542 − 6,209 = 55,333, above the current portion of 50,575. The 2023 figures fit (93,649 − 11,096 = 82,553, below 87,223). PHE does not explain the gap.</p><p>Additions to the liability (82,334) are more than three and a half times additions to the asset (22,519), although under section 4 they normally move together, and PHE does not reconcile the two. The finance cost note reports lease interest of 17,225 (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), against 7,354 here. Its narrative names finance leases of state-owned assets and leases of LPG plants and gas pipelines among the sources of interest, without saying which sit in that line. The state-owned asset leases are carried in "due to the Government", 63,825 at PEP (Note 20, Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131). Lease debt across the two notes is therefore at least 112,926 + 63,825 = 176,751.</p><h2>14. Upstream note: state-owned assets under lease</h2><p>Some of what PHE uses belongs to the state. Assets of the former Pertamina entity that were left out of its opening balance sheet are state property (<em>Barang Milik Negara</em>), and PEP leases them from the Ministry of Finance (Note 20, PDF pp. 131–132; Note 45b, Daftar 5/189, PDF p. 206). Under gross split contracts, assets of earlier PSCs that the state now owns are used through a lease scheme, with rent set on their appraised fair value (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34). Equipment bought under a cost recovery PSC is also state property that the contractor has the right to use (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). Whether each of these arrangements is a lease under PSAK 116, and where it is presented, is a judgment the FS shows only in part.</p><h2>15. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>lease-liabilities-current</code> and <code>lease-liabilities-non-current</code>: the other side of every right-of-use asset, covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>: the depreciation and impairment rules the asset follows, and land rights that are purchases.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-to-government-current</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-to-government-non-current</a>: where the state-owned asset leases sit.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">depreciation-depletion-amortization</a>: where right-of-use depreciation is charged.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>Note 18's correction columns (net +4,950) and the positive 59,120 in the tanks depreciation column are unexplained in the FS. This page does not guess at them; the ratios drawn from the table assume its totals are right.</p></li><li><p>The total lease debt of at least 176,751 adds two notes on the assumption that the state-owned asset leases in Note 20 are leases in the same sense as Note 30. PHE calls them finance leases and does not say whether they are measured under PSAK 116. Note 41's 17,225 of lease interest may include them and third-party LPG plant and pipeline leases; the FS does not split it.</p></li><li><p>The current portion of 50,575 in Note 30 is below the minimum principal due within a year implied by the maturity table (55,333). The page reads the split as principal within twelve months, the usual practice; PHE may define it differently or one figure may be misprinted.</p></li><li><p>The industry descriptions (airline return conditions, tower leases, charter types) are general practice from knowledge of IFRS 16 application, not researched for named Indonesian companies. The treatment of ijarah under PSAK 407 and its interaction with PSAK 116 was not checked.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2 new standards (Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69); Note 3n (Daftar 5/57–5/59, PDF pp. 74–76); Note 18 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132); Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 45b (Daftar 5/189, PDF p. 206). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>The page follows the order of Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition (Wiley), ch. 21 <em>Accounting for Leases</em> (leasing environment, lessee accounting, short-term leases, lessor accounting, special issues, sale and leaseback), and the lessee structure of PSAK 116. The book was not opened for this page; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 116 <em>Sewa</em> (IFRS 16 <em>Leases</em>), paras. 3, 5–9, 12–15, 18–20, 24–25, 26–27, 31–35, 38–50, 53, 61–66, 98–103, B8, B20. VERIFIED (web, IFRS 16 as adopted in Commission Regulation (EU) 2017/1986, legislation.gov.uk). USD 5,000 low-value benchmark (BC100): VERIFIED (web, secondary: KPMG Australia, "AASB 16 Check: short-term and low value exemptions"; BDO UK, "IFRS 16 – a closer look at 'low value'"). 2022 amendment <em>Lease Liability in a Sale and Leaseback</em>: issue VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org project page); purpose as stated in PHE's Note 2; para. 102A not opened. Application guidance on substitution rights (B14–B19) and para. 58 (maturity analysis under PSAK 107) STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. Current/non-current split of the liability under PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–76: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision "Liabilities in relation to a Joint Operator's Interest in a Joint Operation (IFRS 11)", March 2019. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus meeting notes, March 2019; IFRS IC staff paper AP9 on recovery under the joint operating agreement).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 30 (IAS 17, withdrawn when PSAK 73 took effect in 2020, so not on the renumbering list), PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 216 (IAS 16), PSAK 236 (IAS 36), PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 407, ISAK 336. Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36, 2020). Other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Since PSAK 116 a lessee puts almost every lease on its balance sheet as a right-of-use asset and a lease liability. The liability is the present value of the lease payments over the lease term, and the asset is the liability plus prepayments, initial direct costs and restoration costs, less incentives received.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two judgments drive the numbers. One is whether the contract is a lease at all, which turns on who controls an identified asset. The other is the lease term, which includes option periods the lessee is reasonably certain to use.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Afterwards the asset is depreciated, usually straight-line, while interest on the liability falls as it is repaid, so a lease costs more in its early years than the even rent of the old standard. Changes in term, index-linked rent or scope remeasure the liability and move the asset with it.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report right-of-use assets, net, of 123,977 at 31 December 2024 against 203,379 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11). The line fell 39.0 percent and is 0.4 percent of total assets (0.7 percent a year earlier). Against it stand lease liabilities of 112,926, of which 50,575 is current and 62,351 non-current, against 173,189 a year earlier (87,223 and 85,966) (Note 30, Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163). This page covers both liability lines.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should come away able to decide whether a contract holds a lease, measure the asset and liability, run them through their life with journals, and read a lessee's lease notes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 116 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Sewa","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16), which PHE adopted on 1 January 2020 (Note 18, Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Why leases are on the lessee's balance sheet","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the old standard, PSAK 30 (IAS 17), a lessee split leases in two. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"finance lease","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", one that transferred substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership, went on the balance sheet. An ","type":"text"},{"text":"operating lease","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" did not. The lessee expensed the rent and disclosed its commitments in a note. Kieso calls the result off-balance-sheet financing. Two airlines flying identical fleets could show very different debt depending on how their leases were drafted.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116 removes the split for lessees. A lease gives the lessee a right to use an asset, which is an asset, and an obligation to pay for it, which is a liability, whatever the contract is called. For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary adds to its ships and aircraft class (45,240 net at the end of 2024, Note 18) by chartering a supply vessel for three years at 1,000 a year, paid at each year end, and could borrow for three years at 6 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 30, operating lease","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance sheet at the start","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"nothing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"asset and liability of 1,000 × 2.673012 = 2,673.01","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit or loss each year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"rent expense 1,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depreciation plus interest","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.673012","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the annuity factor at 6 percent for three years, (1 − 1.06^−3) / 0.06, the present value of 1 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Is the contract a lease?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A contract is or contains a lease if it conveys the right to control the use of an ","type":"text"},{"text":"identified asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" for a period in exchange for consideration (para. 9). PHE's policy uses the same words (Note 3n, Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74). Control has two parts, tested over the period of use.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identified asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The asset is named or implied in the contract, and the supplier has no ","type":"text"},{"text":"substantive","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" right to substitute another. A substitution right is substantive only if the supplier can practically swap the asset and would benefit from doing so. A right to substitute only when the asset breaks down does not count.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right to control","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The customer obtains substantially all the economic benefits from use, and directs how and for what purpose the asset is used.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the charter names one vessel. The subsidiary decides which platforms it serves, in what order and when it sails, while the owner's crew operates it. The customer directs the use, so the charter contains a lease. If instead a marine contractor agreed to move a set tonnage of cargo each month with whichever boats it chose, the contractor would be directing its own fleet and the subsidiary would be buying a transport service, with nothing on this line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Separating components.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A charter usually pays for the vessel and for services such as crew, fuel management and catering. The consideration is split on relative stand-alone prices, and only the lease component goes into the liability (paras. 12–13). Suppose the day rate is 30, a bareboat charter (the vessel alone, with the charterer finding the crew) would cost 21 a day, and comparable crew services would cost 14.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease component = 30 × 21 / (21 + 14) = 18 a day; annual lease payment = 18 × 365 = 6,570","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-lease component = 30 × 14 / 35 = 12 a day; 12 × 365 = 4,380 a year, expensed as the services are received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A lessee may elect, by class of asset, to treat the whole payment as lease (para. 15), which would put 30 × 365 = 10,950 a year into the liability instead of 6,570.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. The lease term","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"text":"lease term","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the non-cancellable period plus periods under an extension option the lessee is ","type":"text"},{"text":"reasonably certain","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" to exercise, and periods under a termination option it is reasonably certain not to exercise (para. 18). Reasonably certain is a high threshold, above \"more likely than not\". The entity weighs every economic incentive, such as rent in the option period against market rent, significant improvements it has made to the asset, the cost of relocating, and how important the asset is to its operations (para. 19).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the vessel charter runs three years with an option to extend for two more at the same rate. At commencement the subsidiary's field work is contracted for only three years, so the extension is not reasonably certain and the term is three years. The term is reassessed only when a significant event within the lessee's control changes that view (para. 20), which section 8 picks up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Initial measurement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lease liability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is the present value of the lease payments not yet paid at commencement (para. 26). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Lease payments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are (para. 27):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed payments, including payments that are fixed in substance, less incentives receivable;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"variable payments that depend on an index or a rate, measured at the index or rate at commencement;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amounts the lessee expects to pay under a ","type":"text"},{"text":"residual value guarantee","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a promise that the asset will be worth at least a set amount at the end;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the exercise price of a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"penalties for terminating the lease, if the term assumes termination.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The discount rate is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"rate implicit in the lease","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" if it can be readily determined, otherwise the lessee's ","type":"text"},{"text":"incremental borrowing rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", what it would pay to borrow over a similar term with similar security for an asset of similar value. PHE uses the incremental borrowing rate, because the implicit rate is not readily determinable (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The right-of-use asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is the liability plus lease payments made at or before commencement, initial direct costs, and the estimated cost of dismantling or restoring the asset that the lease obliges the lessee to bear, less lease incentives received (para. 24). PHE's policy lists the same components except restoration costs (Note 3n, Daftar 5/58, PDF p. 75).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the vessel charter pays 6,570 a year in arrears for three years at an incremental borrowing rate of 6 percent. The subsidiary pays a broker 120 to arrange it, and the owner pays the subsidiary 150 toward fitting the vessel with its equipment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease liability = 6,570 × 2.673012 = 17,561.69","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use asset = 17,561.69 + 120 initial direct costs − 150 incentive = 17,531.69","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Account","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,561.69","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,561.69","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets (broker's fee)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"120.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"120.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash (incentive received)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"150.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"150.00","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Subsequent measurement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The two sides now follow different rules. The liability is carried at amortised cost, with interest added at the discount rate on the opening balance and payments deducted (the ","type":"text"},{"text":"effective interest method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). The asset is depreciated under PSAK 216's rules, usually straight-line (PHE's policy), from commencement over the shorter of the lease term and its useful life, or over its useful life if ownership passes or a purchase option is reasonably certain (paras. 31–32). It is also tested for impairment under PSAK 236 (para. 33).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest at 6%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest + depreciation","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,561.69","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,053.70","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,570.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,045.39","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,843.90","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,897.60","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,045.39","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"722.72","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,570.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,198.11","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,843.90","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,566.62","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,198.11","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"371.89","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,570.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,843.89","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,215.78","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation = 17,531.69 / 3 = 5,843.90 a year, with 0.01 of rounding in year 3","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total charge = 1,053.70 + 722.72 + 371.89 + 17,531.69 = 19,680.00 = 3 × 6,570 + 120 − 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year-1 journals","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 1,053.70, Dr Lease liabilities 5,516.30 / Cr Cash 6,570.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"payment","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Depreciation expense 5,843.90 / Cr Accumulated depreciation, right-of-use assets 5,843.90","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depreciation","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The total over three years is the same as under PSAK 30, where the cost would have been (19,710 + 120 − 150) / 3 = 6,560 a year. The timing differs. PSAK 116 charges 337.60 more in year 1 and 344.22 less in year 3, because interest is largest when the liability is largest. A portfolio of leases of different ages smooths this out, but a single new lease is front-loaded.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The statements also look different. Depreciation sits in operating expenses and interest in finance costs, so EBITDA (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation) rises when a company moves from PSAK 30 to PSAK 116. In the cash flow statement the principal part of each payment is a financing outflow, and payments for short-term and low-value leases and variable payments outside the liability are operating (para. 50). PHE shows \"payments of lease liabilities\" of 146,158 in financing activities (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Variable payments, guarantees and purchase options","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The standard sorts variable payments by what they depend on.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Index- or rate-linked payments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" go into the liability at the current index, and when the index moves the liability is remeasured at the original discount rate (here 6 percent), with the asset adjusted by the same amount (paras. 39, 42–43). For example (illustration), an office lease pays 500 a year for five years, linked to the consumer price index. After the second payment the index rises 4 percent, so the three remaining payments become 520.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Increase in lease liability = 20 × 2.673012 = 53.46","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Right-of-use assets 53.46 / Cr Lease liabilities 53.46","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Usage- or performance-based payments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", such as a stand-by rate per day a rig waits on weather, or rent as a share of a shop's sales, stay out of the liability and are expensed when they arise (para. 38). PHE's policy says the same (Note 3n, PDF p. 75).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Residual value guarantees","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" enter the liability only at the amount the lessee expects to pay under them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase options","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" reasonably certain to be exercised enter the liability at their exercise price, and the asset is then depreciated over its useful life because the lessee expects to keep it. For example (illustration), a subsidiary leases a vehicle for three years at 30 a year with an option to buy it for 10 at the end, which it is reasonably certain to take. The vehicle will last six years and the incremental borrowing rate is 7 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease liability = 30 × 2.624316 + 10 / 1.07^3 = 78.73 + 8.16 = 86.89","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation = 86.89 / 6 = 14.48 a year, over the useful life","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Short-term and low-value leases","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two exemptions let a lessee skip the balance sheet and expense payments straight-line (paras. 5–8). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"short-term lease","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" has a term of 12 months or less at commencement and no purchase option, and the election is made by class of asset. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"low-value lease","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is judged lease by lease on the value of the asset when new; the IASB had in mind items such as laptops and phones, around USD 5,000 or less in whole dollars. PHE applies both (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76). For example (illustration), a four-month crane hire at 25 a month.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Lease expense, short-term 25 / Cr Cash 25, each month; 100 in total","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A short-term lease that is extended or modified becomes a new lease and is tested again (para. 7).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Reassessments and modifications","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reassessment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" happens when something already in the contract is seen differently, for example when the lessee becomes reasonably certain to use an extension option, or stops being certain. The liability is remeasured with the revised payments at a revised discount rate, the current incremental borrowing rate, and the asset moves by the same amount (paras. 39–41). For example (illustration), at the end of year 1 the subsidiary signs a five-year field contract that needs the vessel, an event within its control that makes the two-year extension reasonably certain. Four payments now remain, and its incremental borrowing rate has risen to 7 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revised liability = 6,570 × 3.387211 = 22,253.98, where 3.387211 = (1 − 1.07^−4) / 0.07","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Increase = 22,253.98 − 12,045.39 = 10,208.59","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use asset = (17,531.69 − 5,843.90) + 10,208.59 = 21,896.38, depreciated over four years at 5,474.10 a year","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Right-of-use assets 10,208.59 / Cr Lease liabilities 10,208.59","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A modification","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is a change in scope or price outside the original terms (para. 44). If it adds the right to use another asset at a price matching its stand-alone price, it is a separate new lease. If it reduces scope, for example by returning a physically distinct asset, the lessee first removes the proportion of asset and liability given up, with the difference in profit or loss, and then remeasures what remains at a revised rate (para. 46(a)). Half of one vessel's capacity would not do, because a capacity portion that is not physically distinct is not an identified asset (para. B20). For example (illustration), suppose the charter had covered two identical vessels, each carrying half the payments, and at the end of year 1 the owner agreed to take one back for the remaining two years. Half of the asset (11,687.79 × 50% = 5,843.90) and half of the liability (12,045.39 × 50% = 6,022.70) are derecognised.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Lease liabilities 6,022.70 / Cr Right-of-use assets 5,843.90 / Cr Gain on lease modification 178.80","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The remaining liability would then be remeasured on the new payments, with the asset adjusted. PHE's policy lists modifications, changes in term or payments and changes in purchase-option assessments as triggers for remeasurement (Note 3n, PDF p. 76).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Impairment, other models and presentation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A right-of-use asset is tested for impairment like any fixed asset, usually within a cash-generating unit (para. 33). If the lessee revalues the class of fixed assets the leased asset belongs to, it may revalue the right-of-use assets in that class too (para. 35). A right-of-use asset that meets the definition of investment property, a building leased and sublet for rent, follows PSAK 240 (para. 34).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets and lease liabilities are presented separately or disclosed by line, and interest on the liability is presented as a finance cost (paras. 47, 49). Disclosure covers depreciation by class, interest, short-term and low-value expense, variable payments, total cash outflow, additions, and carrying amounts by class (para. 53), plus a maturity analysis of the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability is split between current and non-current under PSAK 201 like any borrowing. The current portion is the principal to be repaid within twelve months, not the payments due. For example (illustration), the vessel liability of 12,045.39 at the end of year 1 splits as follows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current = 6,570.00 − 722.72 interest of year 2 = 5,847.28","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current = 12,045.39 − 5,847.28 = 6,198.11, the closing liability of year 2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The maturity analysis shows undiscounted payments (para. 58), so its first bucket, 6,570, exceeds the current portion by the interest still to accrue.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Sale and leaseback","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"sale and leaseback","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" the owner sells an asset and leases it straight back. If the transfer is a sale under PSAK 115, the seller keeps on its books only the part of the old carrying amount that relates to the right of use it retains, and recognises a gain only on the rights actually passed to the buyer (paras. 99–100). If the transfer is not a sale, the asset stays and the cash is a financial liability (para. 103). A 2022 amendment, which PHE notes had no significant effect on it, stops the seller-lessee from measuring the lease liability in a way that recognises gain on the retained right (Note 2, Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary sells an office building with a carrying amount of 600 to an investor for 1,000, its fair value, and leases it back for five years at 80 a year in arrears. The incremental borrowing rate is 6 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease liability = 80 × 4.212364 = 336.99","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use asset = 600 × 336.99 / 1,000 = 202.19, the share of the old carrying amount retained","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total gain = 1,000 − 600 = 400; gain recognised = 400 × (1,000 − 336.99) / 1,000 = 265.20","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Account","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,000.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"202.19","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Building (carrying amount)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"600.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"336.99","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain on sale and leaseback","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"265.20","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The remaining 134.80 of the gain is never booked as such. It is embedded in the lower right-of-use asset and reaches profit through lower depreciation over the five years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. The lessor's side, briefly","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lessors keep the two-model split that lessees lost. A lease that transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership is a finance lease, and the lessor replaces the asset with a receivable. Any other lease is an operating lease, and the lessor keeps the asset and recognises rent straight-line (paras. 61–66). Kieso's indicators are transfer of ownership, a purchase option priced to be exercised, a term covering the major part of the asset's life, and payments with a present value close to its fair value. PHE's lessor policy describes operating leases, with rent recognised straight-line in other operating income (Note 3n, Daftar 5/59, PDF p. 76).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Right-of-use assets across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116 matters most where the business runs on assets it does not own.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Airlines.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Aircraft are the classic leased asset, and many Indonesian carriers lease most of their fleets. Leases often carry ","type":"text"},{"text":"return conditions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", obligations to hand the aircraft back with a set amount of engine and airframe life, which add restoration-type costs to the asset as the obligation arises, with a provision under PSAK 237, and maintenance reserves paid to the lessor during the term.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Telecommunications and towers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Mobile operators lease space on towers, rooftops and land for their sites, often for long terms with renewal options, so the lease term judgment in section 3 drives the liability. The tower companies on the other side are lessors with operating leases.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Retail and banking.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Shops and bank branches are leased buildings. Rent that varies with sales stays out of the liability under section 6, so a retailer on turnover rent carries a smaller liability than one on fixed rent for the same store.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shipping and offshore services.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A ","type":"text"},{"text":"bareboat charter","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (vessel only) is usually a lease. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"time charter","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (vessel with crew, the charterer directing where it goes) usually contains one, with the crew as a non-lease component, as in section 2. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"voyage charter","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", where the owner directs the vessel to deliver a cargo, is usually a service.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land rights.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian land rights such as ","type":"text"},{"text":"hak guna bangunan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are tested under ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36). A right that is in substance a land purchase is a fixed asset, and a right to use is a lease. PHE applies that test (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69) and carries land rights of 127, net, in this line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An ","type":"text"},{"text":"ijarah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the sharia lease, is accounted for under PSAK 407 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Ijarah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 107) where the entity applies the sharia standards (STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream oil and gas.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Leases to explore for or use oil and gas are outside PSAK 116 (para. 3(a)), which covers the right to the resource itself. Rigs, vessels, tanks and buildings used in those operations are inside it. When an operator signs a lease for a joint operation, it recognises the full liability if it is the party the lessor can pursue for payment, and recovers the partners' shares through joint billings (IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision, March 2019).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 18 and 30","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's Note 18 shows cost and accumulated depreciation by class (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). Net book values, computed from that table:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Class","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land rights","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"127","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"176","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Buildings","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14,043","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"22,368","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tanks, pipeline installations and other equipment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"48,839","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"54,960","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ships and aircraft","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"45,240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"56,207","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Movable assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,728","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"69,668","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets, net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"123,977","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"203,379","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"203,379 + additions 22,519 − cost \"correction\" 131,884 − reclassification 450 − depreciation 107,172 + accumulated depreciation \"correction\" 136,834 + reclassification 751 = 123,977","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The correction columns change cost by −131,884 and accumulated depreciation by +136,834, raising net book value by 4,950. They do not offset class by class, and PHE does not explain them. Two ratios are consistent with a portfolio running off.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated depreciation / cost = 901,080 / 1,025,057 = 87.9% (2023: 931,493 / 1,134,872 = 82.1%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Undiscounted payments due within one year = 61,542 / 119,135 = 51.7%; due after five years = 393","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability side reconciles in Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"173,189 + additions 82,334 + interest 7,354 − payments 146,158 − exchange differences 3,793 = 112,926","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied interest rate = 7,354 / ((173,189 + 112,926) / 2) = 5.1%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Undiscounted payments 119,135 − future interest 6,209 = 112,926 = current 50,575 + non-current 62,351","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The split does not fit section 9 on the printed figures. Payments due within one year are 61,542, so even if all 6,209 of future interest fell in that year, principal due within it would be at least 61,542 − 6,209 = 55,333, above the current portion of 50,575. The 2023 figures fit (93,649 − 11,096 = 82,553, below 87,223). PHE does not explain the gap.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Additions to the liability (82,334) are more than three and a half times additions to the asset (22,519), although under section 4 they normally move together, and PHE does not reconcile the two. The finance cost note reports lease interest of 17,225 (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), against 7,354 here. Its narrative names finance leases of state-owned assets and leases of LPG plants and gas pipelines among the sources of interest, without saying which sit in that line. The state-owned asset leases are carried in \"due to the Government\", 63,825 at PEP (Note 20, Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131). Lease debt across the two notes is therefore at least 112,926 + 63,825 = 176,751.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14. Upstream note: state-owned assets under lease","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Some of what PHE uses belongs to the state. Assets of the former Pertamina entity that were left out of its opening balance sheet are state property (","type":"text"},{"text":"Barang Milik Negara","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), and PEP leases them from the Ministry of Finance (Note 20, PDF pp. 131–132; Note 45b, Daftar 5/189, PDF p. 206). Under gross split contracts, assets of earlier PSCs that the state now owns are used through a lease scheme, with rent set on their appraised fair value (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34). Equipment bought under a cost recovery PSC is also state property that the contractor has the right to use (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). Whether each of these arrangements is a lease under PSAK 116, and where it is presented, is a judgment the FS shows only in part.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"lease-liabilities-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"lease-liabilities-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the other side of every right-of-use asset, covered on this page.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the depreciation and impairment rules the asset follows, and land rights that are purchases.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"due-to-government-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"due-to-government-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where the state-owned asset leases sit.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depreciation-depletion-amortization","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where right-of-use depreciation is charged.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 18's correction columns (net +4,950) and the positive 59,120 in the tanks depreciation column are unexplained in the FS. This page does not guess at them; the ratios drawn from the table assume its totals are right.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The total lease debt of at least 176,751 adds two notes on the assumption that the state-owned asset leases in Note 20 are leases in the same sense as Note 30. PHE calls them finance leases and does not say whether they are measured under PSAK 116. Note 41's 17,225 of lease interest may include them and third-party LPG plant and pipeline leases; the FS does not split it.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current portion of 50,575 in Note 30 is below the minimum principal due within a year implied by the maturity table (55,333). The page reads the split as principal within twelve months, the usual practice; PHE may define it differently or one figure may be misprinted.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The industry descriptions (airline return conditions, tower leases, charter types) are general practice from knowledge of IFRS 16 application, not researched for named Indonesian companies. The treatment of ijarah under PSAK 407 and its interaction with PSAK 116 was not checked.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2 new standards (Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69); Note 3n (Daftar 5/57–5/59, PDF pp. 74–76); Note 18 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132); Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 45b (Daftar 5/189, PDF p. 206). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The page follows the order of Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition (Wiley), ch. 21 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accounting for Leases","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (leasing environment, lessee accounting, short-term leases, lessor accounting, special issues, sale and leaseback), and the lessee structure of PSAK 116. The book was not opened for this page; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Sewa","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 16 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Leases","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), paras. 3, 5–9, 12–15, 18–20, 24–25, 26–27, 31–35, 38–50, 53, 61–66, 98–103, B8, B20. VERIFIED (web, IFRS 16 as adopted in Commission Regulation (EU) 2017/1986, legislation.gov.uk). USD 5,000 low-value benchmark (BC100): VERIFIED (web, secondary: KPMG Australia, \"AASB 16 Check: short-term and low value exemptions\"; BDO UK, \"IFRS 16 – a closer look at 'low value'\"). 2022 amendment ","type":"text"},{"text":"Lease Liability in a Sale and Leaseback","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": issue VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org project page); purpose as stated in PHE's Note 2; para. 102A not opened. Application guidance on substitution rights (B14–B19) and para. 58 (maturity analysis under PSAK 107) STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. Current/non-current split of the liability under PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–76: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision \"Liabilities in relation to a Joint Operator's Interest in a Joint Operation (IFRS 11)\", March 2019. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus meeting notes, March 2019; IFRS IC staff paper AP9 on recovery under the joint operating agreement).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 30 (IAS 17, withdrawn when PSAK 73 took effect in 2020, so not on the renumbering list), PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 216 (IAS 16), PSAK 236 (IAS 36), PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 407, ISAK 336. Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36, 2020). Other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of lease accounting for the lessee from identification to disclosure, worked through PHE's leased vessels, tanks and buildings and set beside the industries where leases dominate","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Since PSAK 116 a lessee puts almost every lease on its balance sheet as a right-of-use asset and a lease liability. The liability is the present value of the lease payments over the lease term, and the asset is the liability plus prepayments, initial direct costs and restoration costs, less incentives received.","Two judgments drive the numbers. One is whether the contract is a lease at all, which turns on who controls an identified asset. The other is the lease term, which includes option periods the lessee is reasonably certain to use.","Afterwards the asset is depreciated, usually straight-line, while interest on the liability falls as it is repaid, so a lease costs more in its early years than the even rent of the old standard. Changes in term, index-linked rent or scope remeasure the liability and move the asset with it."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '7f56ddf3-eb7b-4ec4-9216-919e7b1b853c' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
