-- share-capital-contributed-capital-and-common-control
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Equity is a residual, but company law carves out a fixed slice, <em>legal capital</em>, equal to the nominal value of issued shares, which in Indonesia must be paid in full and, for at least 25 percent of authorised capital, at incorporation. Anything paid above nominal is share premium (agio), shown with other contributions as additional paid-in capital.</p></li><li><p>Shares issued for cash are recorded at what is received less issue costs; shares issued for non-cash assets at the fair value of what is received, or of the shares if that is more reliable. Treasury shares and preference shares sit at the border with liabilities. A buyback is deducted from equity and never produces a gain or loss in profit, while a preference share the issuer must redeem is a liability whose dividends are interest.</p></li><li><p>When businesses move between companies under one controlling party, PSAK 338 replaces fair values with carrying amounts (pooling of interests) and books the difference from the consideration in additional paid-in capital. That difference is negative whenever the consideration exceeds the carrying amount taken over.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report share capital of 13,631,466 at both 31 December 2024 and 31 December 2023, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; "Daftar" is the printed page). Beside it sit additional paid-in capital (APIC) of −3,649,862 and an "other equity component" of −4,958 (−11,098 a year earlier). Share capital is 44.8 percent of total assets (44.1 percent in 2023) and 90.2 percent of total equity (96.1 percent).</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to explain what share capital legally is, record share issues, issue costs, preference shares and treasury shares, and apply pooling of interests to a group restructuring.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Rupiah amounts are full rupiah. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. Retained earnings, other comprehensive income (OCI) and non-controlling interest (NCI) have their own essays.</p><h2>1. What share capital is</h2><p>Equity is the residual interest in assets after deducting liabilities (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). By source, it splits into share capital, share premium, retained earnings, accumulated OCI, treasury shares (a deduction) and NCI.</p><p>The first two are <em>contributed capital</em>, what owners paid in. Retained earnings are <em>earned capital</em>, what the entity generated and kept; this page also counts accumulated OCI there. On PHE's 2024 figures:</p><blockquote><p>Contributed = 13,631,466 − 3,649,862 = 9,981,604 (69.2% of equity attributable to owners of 14,433,117)</p><p>Earned = 1,167,892 + 3,184,728 + 103,851 = 4,456,471 (30.9%); the other equity component of −4,958 closes the total</p></blockquote><p>Company law locks part of contributed capital in, through three amounts in UU 40/2007 on Limited Liability Companies (UU PT).</p><ul><li><p><em>Authorised capital</em> (modal dasar) is the nominal value of all shares the articles of association allow (art. 31(1)). Authorisation creates no asset and no claim, so it needs no journal entry, only a memorandum.</p></li><li><p><em>Issued capital</em> (modal ditempatkan) is the nominal value of shares actually taken up.</p></li><li><p><em>Paid-up capital</em> (modal disetor) is what has been paid for them. At least 25 percent of authorised capital must be issued and fully paid (art. 33(1)), and every later issue must be paid in full (art. 33(3)); the elucidation adds that instalments are not possible. Issued and paid-up are therefore the same number, and Kieso's <em>subscriptions receivable</em> hardly arises.</p></li></ul><p>Cipta Kerja replaced art. 32's minimum authorised capital of Rp50,000,000 with an amount the founders decide (art. 109 point 3 of Perppu 2/2022, enacted as UU 6/2023; PP 8/2021), leaving art. 33 unchanged.</p><p>PHE's figures fit the rule (Note 32, Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164). Authorised capital is Rp790,000,000,000,000, divided into 1,580,000,000,000 shares of Rp500.</p><blockquote><p>Issued = 396,225,242,340 shares × Rp500 = Rp198,112,621,170,000</p><p>Issued / authorised = 198,112,621,170,000 / 790,000,000,000,000 = 25.08%</p></blockquote><p>PT Pertamina (Persero) holds 99.9968 percent and PT Pertamina Pedeve Indonesia 0.0032 percent.</p><p>Each share gives the right to vote at the general meeting of shareholders (RUPS) and to receive dividends and remaining assets in liquidation (art. 52(1)); textbooks also list a pre-emptive right to new shares.</p><h2>2. Par value, no-par shares and the stock split</h2><p><em>Par value</em> (nominal value) is a legal amount unrelated to fair value. It marks out legal capital, which the company may return to shareholders only through procedures the law sets, such as a capital reduction or a limited buyback (section 6). <em>No-par</em> shares have no such amount; some jurisdictions then require a <em>stated value</em>.</p><p>Indonesia requires par. Share values must be stated in rupiah and no-par shares cannot be issued (UU PT art. 49(1)–(2)), unless capital-market legislation provides otherwise (arts. 31(2) and 49(3)). When shares are sold for more than nominal, the excess is <em>agio</em> (share premium), recorded in the statements as such (elucidation to art. 8(2)(c)). Indonesian statements present it within additional paid-in capital (tambahan modal disetor).</p><p>An issue below par, recorded for example (illustration) as "Assets/service $40,000 / PIC – COMMON STOCK $10,000 / Common stock $50,000", has no Indonesian counterpart. Issued capital is the nominal value and must be paid in full, so a share cannot be paid for with less than its nominal value. The standard-setter, DSAK IAI, reads the law as prohibiting <em>disagio</em> on the initial payment of capital (PSAK 338 Basis for Conclusions, DK10).</p><p><strong>A rupiah par in a dollar entity.</strong> PHE's functional currency is the US dollar (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83), but its shares carry a rupiah par. Contributed equity is not a monetary item, so it is translated once, at the rate on the contribution date. For example (illustration), a subsidiary with a USD functional currency issues 1,000,000,000 shares of Rp500 nominal for Rp600 each when the rate is Rp15,000 per USD.</p><blockquote><p>Cash = 1,000,000,000 × Rp600 / 15,000 / 1,000 = 40,000</p><p>Share capital = 1,000,000,000 × Rp500 / 15,000 / 1,000 = 33,333.33; share premium = 6,666.67</p><p>Dr Cash 40,000 / Cr Share capital 33,333.33 / Cr Share premium 6,666.67</p></blockquote><p>A later move in the rate is not booked. PHE's share capital carries at least two historical rates, each an average of the issues behind it.</p><blockquote><p>Pertamina: Rp198,106,221,170,000 / 13,630,780 thousand = Rp14,533.7 per USD</p><p>Pedeve: 12,800,000 × Rp500 = Rp6,400,000,000 / 686 thousand = Rp9,329.4 per USD</p></blockquote><p><strong>Stock splits.</strong> A split cuts the par in proportion to the new number of shares. Nothing moves between equity accounts, so it needs only a memorandum entry. PHE split its shares from Rp2,500 to Rp500 by resolution of an extraordinary RUPS on 2 November 2022 (Note 32).</p><blockquote><p>Shares before = 396,225,242,340 / 5 = 79,245,048,468 at Rp2,500; after = 396,225,242,340 at Rp500</p><p>Issued capital = Rp198,112,621,170,000 before and after</p></blockquote><h2>3. Issuing shares for cash</h2><p>A cash issue is recorded at what is received: nominal to share capital, the rest to share premium.</p><p><strong>Issue costs.</strong> Underwriting, legal, accounting and printing fees for new shares are costs of raising equity. PSAK 232 <em>Financial Instruments: Presentation</em> (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) deducts them from equity, net of any related income tax (paras. 35A, 37). For example (illustration), a USD-functional subsidiary raising rupiah lists 2,000,000,000 new shares of Rp500 nominal at Rp1,000 when the rate is Rp16,000 per USD, and pays 2,500 of issue costs.</p><blockquote><p>Gross proceeds = 2,000,000,000 × Rp1,000 / 16,000 / 1,000 = 125,000; share capital = 62,500; share premium = 62,500</p><p>Dr Cash 125,000 / Cr Share capital 62,500 / Cr Share premium 62,500</p><p>Dr Share premium 2,500 / Cr Cash 2,500; net premium = 60,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Shares sold in a package.</strong> A price for ordinary and preference shares sold together is split by relative fair value (<em>proportional method</em>) or, if only one fair value is known, by giving that class its fair value and the other the rest (<em>incremental method</em>). For example (illustration), a package sells for 11,000; the ordinary shares are worth 7,200 and the preference shares 4,800.</p><blockquote><p>Proportional: ordinary = 11,000 × 7,200 / 12,000 = 6,600; preference = 4,400. Incremental (preference value unknown): preference = 11,000 − 7,200 = 3,800</p></blockquote><h2>4. Issuing shares for non-cash assets (inbreng)</h2><p>Shares paid for with land, patents, services or shares in another company are an <em>inbreng</em>. A contribution in another form is valued at fair value, set by market price or by an expert unaffiliated with the company, and a contribution of immovable property must be announced in a newspaper within 14 days (UU PT art. 34(1)–(3)).</p><p>Kieso records the asset at the fair value of what is received, or at the fair value of the shares if that is clearer, and never at book value or nominal. For example (illustration), a regional government company contributes land appraised at 12,000 for new shares with a nominal value equivalent to 9,000.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Land 12,000 / Cr Share capital 9,000 / Cr Share premium 3,000</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), a patent with an independent valuation of USD 125,000 is acquired for 10,000 shares of USD 10 par. Share capital is always the nominal, 10,000 × 10 = 100,000, so the premium is 25,000.</p><p>A business contributed by the receiving company's own controlling party is the exception, because PSAK 338 (section 7) replaces fair value with carrying amount.</p><h2>5. Preference shares</h2><p>A <em>preference share</em> ranks ahead of ordinary shares for dividends, for assets in liquidation, or both. UU PT lets the articles create classes with or without votes, redeemable or convertible after a period, and with cumulative or non-cumulative dividend priority or liquidation priority, one of them designated ordinary (art. 53(3)–(4)). Having no votes is a common feature, not a legal rule.</p><p>Redeemable preference shares resemble bonds, and PSAK 232 decides by substance, not name. An instrument is a financial liability if the issuer has a contractual obligation to deliver cash, such as a mandatory redemption on a fixed date or at the holder's option (para. 18(a)). It is equity if dividends and redemption are at the issuer's discretion. For example (illustration), a subsidiary issues two classes of 10,000 each with a 6 percent dividend.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Class A: cumulative, never redeemable, dividend declared at RUPS discretion</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Class B: mandatorily redeemable at par after five years, dividend mandatory</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Classification</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Equity</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financial liability</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Issue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Share capital, preference 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Redeemable preference shares 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Annual dividend of 600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Retained earnings 600 / Cr Dividends payable 600, only when declared</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Cash 600</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Cumulative</em> means unpaid dividends carry forward and must be paid before ordinary dividends. Arrears are not dividends payable. No liability exists until the RUPS declares a dividend, so arrears on Class A are disclosed, not accrued. A share redeemable only at the issuer's option, with discretionary dividends, stays equity. A <em>participating</em> share also shares in profits beyond its fixed rate; a <em>convertible</em> one can be exchanged for ordinary shares.</p><h2>6. Treasury shares</h2><p>Shares a company buys back are <em>treasury shares</em>. The purchase returns capital to the sellers, so PSAK 232 deducts their cost from equity and records no gain or loss in profit or loss on their purchase, sale, issue or cancellation (para. 33).</p><p>Indonesian law adds its own limits. A buyback must not reduce net assets below issued capital plus the mandatory reserve already set aside, and the nominal value of shares bought back must not exceed 10 percent of issued capital unless capital-market rules say otherwise (art. 37(1)). Treasury shares may be held for at most three years (art. 37(4)), need RUPS approval (art. 38), and carry no votes and no dividends (art. 40).</p><p>The cost method, which most companies use, debits Treasury shares at cost and puts the result of a resale in equity. For example (illustration), a listed subsidiary buys back shares for 900, then resells half for 520 and the other half for 400.</p><blockquote><p>Purchase: Dr Treasury shares 900 / Cr Cash 900</p><p>First resale: Dr Cash 520 / Cr Treasury shares 450 / Cr Share premium, treasury shares 70</p><p>Second resale: Dr Cash 400, Dr Share premium, treasury shares 50 / Cr Treasury shares 450; balance left = 70 − 50 = 20</p></blockquote><p>Had the second resale come first, with no treasury-share premium to absorb it, the 50 would be debited to retained earnings. Profit is unaffected either way.</p><p>The net-asset test compares net assets in the company's latest statements approved by the RUPS with issued capital plus the mandatory reserve, and the law states issued capital in rupiah. For example (illustration), a company with issued capital of Rp100 billion and a mandatory reserve of Rp20 billion has net assets of Rp150 billion, of which Rp30 billion is the negative balance of a common-control difference.</p><blockquote><p>Headroom = 150 − (100 + 20) = Rp30 billion; without the negative APIC it would be Rp60 billion</p></blockquote><p>For PHE the test cannot be run from the published statements. It applies to the company's own net assets, PHE publishes only consolidated figures, and its year-end rate is disclosed only as USD 0.06 per Rp1,000 (Note 3r). The consolidated figures show only the direction. In USD at historical rates, the negative APIC of 3,649,862 offsets most of the retained earnings of 4,352,620.</p><h2>7. Common-control restructurings: PSAK 338</h2><p><strong>The problem.</strong> If each transfer of a subsidiary within a group were an acquisition under PSAK 103 <em>Business Combinations</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3), the receiving company would remeasure everything at fair value and recognise goodwill, though nothing left the group and no outsider set a price. PSAK 103 excludes combinations of entities under common control from its scope, and IFRS has no standard for them, so Indonesia keeps its own standard, PSAK 338 (2012) <em>Business Combinations of Entities under Common Control</em> (formerly PSAK 38, renumbered from 1 January 2024). PHE's 2024 statements apply this version; a 2025 revision, effective 2026, is described below.</p><p><strong>The principle.</strong> A transfer of a business within a group to reorganise it is not a change of ownership in economic substance, so it can create no profit or loss for the group or for any entity in it (para. 07). One example is a parent transferring a subsidiary to another subsidiary it owns (para. 08(b)). Buying out non-controlling shareholders is excluded, because ownership does change (para. 09). The receiving entity uses the <em>pooling-of-interests method</em> at carrying amounts (para. 10), and:</p><ul><li><p>recognises the difference between the consideration and the carrying amount of the business in equity, presented in APIC (para. 11);</p></li><li><p>presents the current and comparative periods as if the combination had happened from the start of common control (para. 12);</p></li><li><p>expenses the combination's costs, except costs of issuing debt or equity, which follow PSAK 109 and PSAK 232 (para. 14).</p></li></ul><p>The transferring entity books its own difference, between the consideration received and the carrying amount it gives up, in APIC too (para. 15). These APIC balances are never recycled to profit or reclassified to retained earnings, even if the business later leaves the group (Basis for Conclusions DK12–DK14). PSAK 38 (2004) had kept the difference in a separate account (SNTRES) and released it to profit when common control ended; its balances moved to APIC on 1 January 2013 (para. 20).</p><p><strong>An example.</strong> For example (illustration, sized to sit close to PHE's APIC of −3,649,862; PHE does not disclose its own combinations' figures), a parent transfers 100 percent of an upstream company to its subholding. The company's net assets have a carrying amount of 6,000,000; the fair value of its identifiable net assets is 8,500,000; an unaffiliated appraiser values the business at 9,650,000. The subholding pays by issuing new shares with a nominal value of 9,650,000, which art. 34 allows because the contribution is valued at 9,650,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Acquisition method (PSAK 103, if the parties were unrelated)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pooling of interests (PSAK 338)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net assets recognised</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,500,000 at fair value</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000,000 at carrying amount</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,650,000 − 8,500,000 = 1,150,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>None</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Difference</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>None</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,650,000 − 6,000,000 = 3,650,000, debit to APIC</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Prior periods</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not restated; results from acquisition date</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Restated as if combined since common control began</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal (consolidated)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Net assets 8,500,000, Dr Goodwill 1,150,000 / Cr Share capital 9,650,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Net assets 6,000,000, Dr APIC 3,650,000 / Cr Share capital 9,650,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Company law values the contribution at fair value and lets the nominal value of shares issued for it go up to that amount, never above it, with any excess of value over nominal recorded as agio. PSAK 338 puts the asset side at book value. When the parties set the nominal near fair value, or pay cash above book value, the gap is a debit, a negative APIC. DSAK IAI answered the objection that this looks like disagio. The legal ban concerns only the initial payment of capital, and APIC holds more than agio, so a negative common-control difference may sit there (DK10).</p><p>The parent books the mirror image (Dr Investment in subholding 9,650,000 / Cr Investment in subsidiary 6,000,000 / Cr APIC 3,650,000), and on consolidation at the ultimate parent the two APIC entries cancel.</p><p><strong>Whose carrying amounts.</strong> The 2012 text, which rejected push-down accounting (DK19–DK22), points to the carrying amounts in the <em>transferred entity's</em> own statements, so goodwill or fair-value uplifts the parent recognised when it first bought the business stay behind. PHE's policy says "the book value of the net assets of the acquiree" (Note 3d), though IAI found practice divided (DK23A of the 2025 exposure draft). The 2025 revision (approved 29 October 2025, effective 1 January 2026, prospective) switches to the <em>transferring entity's</em> carrying amounts unless impracticable and allows impracticable restatement to be skipped (exposure draft paras. 10A–10C, 12A–12B).</p><p><strong>Restating comparatives.</strong> Indonesian practice shows the equity of a business not yet legally owned in a separate column of the statement of changes in equity, closed against share capital and APIC when the transfer is completed (practice, not the standard's text). PHE's statement of changes in equity has such a column, "Penggabungan Entitas/Merging Entities", which is nil at every date shown (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16), consistent with no common-control combination in progress in 2023 or 2024. It matters for an upstream group because Note 1b lists PHE's subsidiaries largely as one company per working area (Daftar 5/4, PDF p. 21), so blocks change hands inside the Pertamina group by moving shares.</p><h2>8. Other components of contributed capital</h2><p>APIC can also hold contributions with no shares issued, such as the benefit of a below-market shareholder loan (see the Long-term bank loans essay). Other equity lines hold items that are neither profit nor OCI: changes in a parent's stake in a subsidiary without loss of control (PSAK 110, formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10), share-based payment reserves (PSAK 102), and the equity component of convertible bonds. PSAK 201 asks for the nature and purpose of each reserve and a reconciliation of every component.</p><h2>9. Share capital across industries</h2><p><strong>Banks and insurers.</strong> PP 8/2021, which implements the amended art. 32, keeps the higher minimums that sector rules set for certain businesses (art. 5); before Cipta Kerja the elucidation to art. 32(2) named banking, insurance and freight forwarding. Commercial banks had to hold core capital (paid-up capital plus reserves and retained earnings) of at least Rp3 trillion by the end of 2022 under POJK 12/POJK.03/2020. Insurers face a minimum equity under POJK 23/2023. For both, the regulator's capital measure is wider than legal capital, and a new licence also needs a minimum paid-up capital.</p><p><strong>State-owned enterprises.</strong> The state funds a Persero by <em>penyertaan modal negara</em> (state equity participation), in cash or in kind, and builds holdings by inbreng of state shares. Government Regulation 6/2018 added to the state's equity in Pertamina by contributing the state's 13,809,038,755 Series B shares in Perusahaan Gas Negara, with the value set by the Minister of Finance; the state kept control through the Series A Dwiwarna share, a single share with special rights.</p><p><strong>Listed companies.</strong> Capital-market law can override UU PT on buyback limits (art. 37(1)(b)) and on no-par shares (art. 49(3)). Listed companies raise equity through rights issues (<em>hak memesan efek terlebih dahulu</em>, the pre-emptive right in tradeable form), whose costs follow section 3.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 31 and 32 and the statement of changes in equity</h2><p>PHE's equity components and their 2024 movements (statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; statement of changes in equity, Daftar 3, PDF p. 16):</p><table style="min-width: 175px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 Jan 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dividends</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Reserve transfer</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Equity adjustment in subsidiaries</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Comprehensive income</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Share capital</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>13,631,466</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>13,631,466</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>APIC</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,649,862)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,649,862)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other equity component</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(11,098)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,140</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4,958)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Retained earnings, appropriated</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>647,955</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>519,937</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,167,892</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Retained earnings, unappropriated</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,782,941</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,079,749)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(519,937)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,001,475</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,184,728</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>OCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>132,724</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(28,873)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>103,851</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Owners of the parent</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>13,534,126</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(2,079,749)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>–</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>6,138</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,972,602</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>14,433,117</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>644,429</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(27,797)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(53,470)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>111,073</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>674,235</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total equity</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>14,178,555</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(2,107,546)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>–</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(47,332)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,083,675</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>15,107,352</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every row and column reconciles. Share capital has not moved since at least the end of 2022; APIC moved only by 7 in 2023.</p><p><strong>What Note 31 says.</strong> APIC "represent[s] the difference between the purchase consideration and the carrying value from business combination transaction of entities under common control" (Note 31, Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163), and the policy applies pooling of interests with the difference in APIC (Note 3d, Daftar 5/37, PDF p. 54). It gives no transactions, dates or amounts. The likely source is the subholding restructuring. Pertamina designated PHE as parent of the Upstream Subholding in June 2020 (Note 1a, Daftar 5/2, PDF p. 19), and by a circular shareholders' resolution of 1 September 2021 PHE took over the shares Pertamina held in its upstream subsidiaries, eleven according to PHE's own publications, including, by one press list, Elnusa, whose 41.1 percent moved that day; PHE bought a further 9.99 percent from Dana Pensiun Pertamina in December 2021, reaching the 51.10 percent in Note 1b. PHE does not disclose the consideration. Shares issued at a nominal value above the transferred carrying amounts, or cash above them, would both produce the negative balance; the §7 mechanism is an inference from Notes 31 and 3d, not a disclosed fact.</p><p><strong>Book value per share and legal headroom.</strong></p><blockquote><p>Book value per share = 14,433,117 thousand / 396,225,242,340 = USD 0.0364</p><p>Share capital per share at historical rates = 13,631,466 thousand / 396,225,242,340 = USD 0.0344</p></blockquote><p>Equity per share is only 6 percent above what the shares were paid in at, the same squeeze section 6 described.</p><p><strong>The other equity component.</strong> PHE gives no note. The statement of changes in equity labels every movement "Equity adjustment in subsidiaries": −23,459 in 2023, with no effect on NCI, and +6,140 in 2024, in the same row as −53,470 against NCI. The consolidation policy treats changes in ownership of a subsidiary without loss of control as equity transactions (Note 3c, Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51), which fits the label, but no transaction behind the 2024 row is disclosed. The Non-controlling interest essay analyses that movement.</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>additional-paid-in-capital</code>: share premium and common-control differences, covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><code>other-equity-component</code>: equity adjustments in subsidiaries, covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings">retained-earnings</a>: dividends, the statutory reserve and the reserve transfer in the table above.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">other-comprehensive-income</a>: the accumulated OCI of 103,851.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">non-controlling-interest</a>: the 674,235 and the 53,470 adjustment.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">long-term-investments</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net">intangible-assets-net</a>: investments in associates and goodwill from acquisitions that are not under common control.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PHE's APIC arose mainly from the 2021 subholding restructuring is inferred. Note 31 gives only the definition, the FS cover 2023–2024, and the transfer of eleven subsidiaries and Elnusa comes from PHE publications and press reports, not the audited statements. UU PT does not force a negative difference: nominal value may be set anywhere up to the contribution's fair value, so the negative balance reflects a consideration (shares or cash) the parties set above the transferred carrying amounts.</p></li><li><p>The reading that UU PT bars issuing shares below nominal rests on art. 33 (full payment of issued capital) and DSAK IAI's Basis for Conclusions DK10, not on an article that says so in terms.</p></li><li><p>The buyback net-asset test is not run for PHE. The law states capital in rupiah and tests the company's own net assets; PHE publishes neither separate statements nor a precise year-end rate. Translated at a year-end rate the rupiah value of USD net assets has risen since 2021 while rupiah capital is fixed, so a USD comparison at historical rates would mislead. Note 33's Indonesian title ("SALDO CADANGAN WAJIB") treats the whole appropriation as the mandatory reserve, while the statement of changes in equity adds "general and investment reserve".</p></li><li><p>The reading of the other equity component as equity transactions in subsidiaries rests on its label and Note 3c. PHE does not describe it, which falls short of PSAK 201's request to describe each reserve. The 2024 investing receipt of 64,578 and Note 42's gain of 34,242 on sale of subsidiaries more likely relate to TPDC's January 2024 exercise of its call option over the Mnazi Bay interest (Note 5g) than to this row; PHE does not connect them, and a sale to an outsider would not reduce NCI.</p></li><li><p>PHE inconsistencies not used in the body: APIC, which Note 31 defines as common-control differences only, moved by 7 in 2023 in the "equity adjustment in subsidiaries" row; Note 32 describes issued capital as 25 percent of authorised capital where it is 25.08 percent.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position, equity (Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15); statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16); Note 1a–1b (Daftar 5/1–5/4, PDF pp. 18–21); Note 3c (Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51); Note 3d (Daftar 5/37, PDF p. 54); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 31 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163); Note 32 (Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164); Note 33 (Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk). Note 52 (subsequent events) contains no equity transactions.</p></li><li><p>UU No. 40 Tahun 2007 tentang Perseroan Terbatas, arts. 31–38, 40, 49, 52–53, 70 and elucidations to arts. 8(2)(c), 32(2), 33(2)–(3), 34. VERIFIED (web, JDIH BPK, peraturan.bpk.go.id, original text; status page lists amendments by UU 11/2020, Perppu 2/2022 and UU 6/2023). Amended art. 32: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Hukumonline, "Modal PT", 18 January 2024, citing art. 109 point 3 Perppu 2/2022 and PP 8/2021); the amending text itself not opened. PP 8/2021 art. 5 (sector minimums): VERIFIED (web, secondary: Hukumonline).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 338 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis Entitas Sepengendali</em> (formerly PSAK 38): renumbering VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list). Paras. 07–12, 14–15, 17–20 and DK08–DK14 of the 2012 text, and DK19–DK23A, VERIFIED (web, IAI, exposure draft PSAK 338 (Revisi 2025), which shows the 2012 text with tracked changes). Measurement at the transferred entity's carrying amounts VERIFIED (web, IAI, Discussion Paper PIR PSAK 338, 2024, paras. 19, 30–34, 36–41). 2025 revision approved 29 October 2025, effective 1 January 2026: VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>DSAK Terkini</em> vol. 7, 2025); final text not opened, so paragraph numbers are the exposure draft's.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 (IAS 32), paras. 18(a), 33, 35A, 37; PSAK 201 (IAS 1) equity disclosures; PSAK 103 (IFRS 3) para. 02(c) scope exclusion VERIFIED (web, quoted in IAI Discussion Paper PIR PSAK 338, footnote 1); PSAK 110 (IFRS 10); PSAK 102 (IFRS 2); PSAK 221 (IAS 21) on historical rates for equity: numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>POJK 12/POJK.03/2020 on bank consolidation, minimum core capital Rp3 trillion by 31 December 2022: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan; Bisnis.com, 2020–2022). POJK 23/2023 insurance minimum equity: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Hukumonline) for its existence only. PP 6/2018 on Pertamina's state equity and PGN Series B shares: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan, Hukumonline, Katadata, March–April 2018).</p></li><li><p>PHE subholding formation (circular resolution of 1 September 2021, eleven subsidiaries) and the Elnusa transfer: VERIFIED (web, secondary: PHE Sustainability Report 2021 and company profile as quoted in search results; CNBC Indonesia, 6 September 2021). Further 9.99 percent from Dana Pensiun Pertamina, December 2021: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan; Bisnis, 3 December 2021). Note 5g (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105), Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167) and the cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17) read for the other equity component. PHE's own pages could not be opened directly.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, ch. 15. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity is a residual, but company law carves out a fixed slice, ","type":"text"},{"text":"legal capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", equal to the nominal value of issued shares, which in Indonesia must be paid in full and, for at least 25 percent of authorised capital, at incorporation. Anything paid above nominal is share premium (agio), shown with other contributions as additional paid-in capital.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shares issued for cash are recorded at what is received less issue costs; shares issued for non-cash assets at the fair value of what is received, or of the shares if that is more reliable. Treasury shares and preference shares sit at the border with liabilities. A buyback is deducted from equity and never produces a gain or loss in profit, while a preference share the issuer must redeem is a liability whose dividends are interest.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When businesses move between companies under one controlling party, PSAK 338 replaces fair values with carrying amounts (pooling of interests) and books the difference from the consideration in additional paid-in capital. That difference is negative whenever the consideration exceeds the carrying amount taken over.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report share capital of 13,631,466 at both 31 December 2024 and 31 December 2023, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). Beside it sit additional paid-in capital (APIC) of −3,649,862 and an \"other equity component\" of −4,958 (−11,098 a year earlier). Share capital is 44.8 percent of total assets (44.1 percent in 2023) and 90.2 percent of total equity (96.1 percent).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to explain what share capital legally is, record share issues, issue costs, preference shares and treasury shares, and apply pooling of interests to a group restructuring.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Rupiah amounts are full rupiah. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. Retained earnings, other comprehensive income (OCI) and non-controlling interest (NCI) have their own essays.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What share capital is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity is the residual interest in assets after deducting liabilities (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). By source, it splits into share capital, share premium, retained earnings, accumulated OCI, treasury shares (a deduction) and NCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The first two are ","type":"text"},{"text":"contributed capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", what owners paid in. Retained earnings are ","type":"text"},{"text":"earned capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", what the entity generated and kept; this page also counts accumulated OCI there. On PHE's 2024 figures:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contributed = 13,631,466 − 3,649,862 = 9,981,604 (69.2% of equity attributable to owners of 14,433,117)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Earned = 1,167,892 + 3,184,728 + 103,851 = 4,456,471 (30.9%); the other equity component of −4,958 closes the total","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Company law locks part of contributed capital in, through three amounts in UU 40/2007 on Limited Liability Companies (UU PT).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Authorised capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (modal dasar) is the nominal value of all shares the articles of association allow (art. 31(1)). Authorisation creates no asset and no claim, so it needs no journal entry, only a memorandum.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issued capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (modal ditempatkan) is the nominal value of shares actually taken up.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid-up capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (modal disetor) is what has been paid for them. At least 25 percent of authorised capital must be issued and fully paid (art. 33(1)), and every later issue must be paid in full (art. 33(3)); the elucidation adds that instalments are not possible. Issued and paid-up are therefore the same number, and Kieso's ","type":"text"},{"text":"subscriptions receivable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" hardly arises.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cipta Kerja replaced art. 32's minimum authorised capital of Rp50,000,000 with an amount the founders decide (art. 109 point 3 of Perppu 2/2022, enacted as UU 6/2023; PP 8/2021), leaving art. 33 unchanged.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's figures fit the rule (Note 32, Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164). Authorised capital is Rp790,000,000,000,000, divided into 1,580,000,000,000 shares of Rp500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issued = 396,225,242,340 shares × Rp500 = Rp198,112,621,170,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issued / authorised = 198,112,621,170,000 / 790,000,000,000,000 = 25.08%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina (Persero) holds 99.9968 percent and PT Pertamina Pedeve Indonesia 0.0032 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each share gives the right to vote at the general meeting of shareholders (RUPS) and to receive dividends and remaining assets in liquidation (art. 52(1)); textbooks also list a pre-emptive right to new shares.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Par value, no-par shares and the stock split","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Par value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (nominal value) is a legal amount unrelated to fair value. It marks out legal capital, which the company may return to shareholders only through procedures the law sets, such as a capital reduction or a limited buyback (section 6). ","type":"text"},{"text":"No-par","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" shares have no such amount; some jurisdictions then require a ","type":"text"},{"text":"stated value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesia requires par. Share values must be stated in rupiah and no-par shares cannot be issued (UU PT art. 49(1)–(2)), unless capital-market legislation provides otherwise (arts. 31(2) and 49(3)). When shares are sold for more than nominal, the excess is ","type":"text"},{"text":"agio","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (share premium), recorded in the statements as such (elucidation to art. 8(2)(c)). Indonesian statements present it within additional paid-in capital (tambahan modal disetor).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An issue below par, recorded for example (illustration) as \"Assets/service $40,000 / PIC – COMMON STOCK $10,000 / Common stock $50,000\", has no Indonesian counterpart. Issued capital is the nominal value and must be paid in full, so a share cannot be paid for with less than its nominal value. The standard-setter, DSAK IAI, reads the law as prohibiting ","type":"text"},{"text":"disagio","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" on the initial payment of capital (PSAK 338 Basis for Conclusions, DK10).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A rupiah par in a dollar entity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE's functional currency is the US dollar (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83), but its shares carry a rupiah par. Contributed equity is not a monetary item, so it is translated once, at the rate on the contribution date. For example (illustration), a subsidiary with a USD functional currency issues 1,000,000,000 shares of Rp500 nominal for Rp600 each when the rate is Rp15,000 per USD.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash = 1,000,000,000 × Rp600 / 15,000 / 1,000 = 40,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share capital = 1,000,000,000 × Rp500 / 15,000 / 1,000 = 33,333.33; share premium = 6,666.67","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 40,000 / Cr Share capital 33,333.33 / Cr Share premium 6,666.67","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A later move in the rate is not booked. PHE's share capital carries at least two historical rates, each an average of the issues behind it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pertamina: Rp198,106,221,170,000 / 13,630,780 thousand = Rp14,533.7 per USD","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pedeve: 12,800,000 × Rp500 = Rp6,400,000,000 / 686 thousand = Rp9,329.4 per USD","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stock splits.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A split cuts the par in proportion to the new number of shares. Nothing moves between equity accounts, so it needs only a memorandum entry. PHE split its shares from Rp2,500 to Rp500 by resolution of an extraordinary RUPS on 2 November 2022 (Note 32).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shares before = 396,225,242,340 / 5 = 79,245,048,468 at Rp2,500; after = 396,225,242,340 at Rp500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issued capital = Rp198,112,621,170,000 before and after","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Issuing shares for cash","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A cash issue is recorded at what is received: nominal to share capital, the rest to share premium.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issue costs.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Underwriting, legal, accounting and printing fees for new shares are costs of raising equity. PSAK 232 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments: Presentation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) deducts them from equity, net of any related income tax (paras. 35A, 37). For example (illustration), a USD-functional subsidiary raising rupiah lists 2,000,000,000 new shares of Rp500 nominal at Rp1,000 when the rate is Rp16,000 per USD, and pays 2,500 of issue costs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross proceeds = 2,000,000,000 × Rp1,000 / 16,000 / 1,000 = 125,000; share capital = 62,500; share premium = 62,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 125,000 / Cr Share capital 62,500 / Cr Share premium 62,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Share premium 2,500 / Cr Cash 2,500; net premium = 60,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shares sold in a package.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A price for ordinary and preference shares sold together is split by relative fair value (","type":"text"},{"text":"proportional method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") or, if only one fair value is known, by giving that class its fair value and the other the rest (","type":"text"},{"text":"incremental method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). For example (illustration), a package sells for 11,000; the ordinary shares are worth 7,200 and the preference shares 4,800.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportional: ordinary = 11,000 × 7,200 / 12,000 = 6,600; preference = 4,400. Incremental (preference value unknown): preference = 11,000 − 7,200 = 3,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Issuing shares for non-cash assets (inbreng)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shares paid for with land, patents, services or shares in another company are an ","type":"text"},{"text":"inbreng","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". A contribution in another form is valued at fair value, set by market price or by an expert unaffiliated with the company, and a contribution of immovable property must be announced in a newspaper within 14 days (UU PT art. 34(1)–(3)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso records the asset at the fair value of what is received, or at the fair value of the shares if that is clearer, and never at book value or nominal. For example (illustration), a regional government company contributes land appraised at 12,000 for new shares with a nominal value equivalent to 9,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Land 12,000 / Cr Share capital 9,000 / Cr Share premium 3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a patent with an independent valuation of USD 125,000 is acquired for 10,000 shares of USD 10 par. Share capital is always the nominal, 10,000 × 10 = 100,000, so the premium is 25,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A business contributed by the receiving company's own controlling party is the exception, because PSAK 338 (section 7) replaces fair value with carrying amount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Preference shares","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"preference share","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" ranks ahead of ordinary shares for dividends, for assets in liquidation, or both. UU PT lets the articles create classes with or without votes, redeemable or convertible after a period, and with cumulative or non-cumulative dividend priority or liquidation priority, one of them designated ordinary (art. 53(3)–(4)). Having no votes is a common feature, not a legal rule.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Redeemable preference shares resemble bonds, and PSAK 232 decides by substance, not name. An instrument is a financial liability if the issuer has a contractual obligation to deliver cash, such as a mandatory redemption on a fixed date or at the holder's option (para. 18(a)). It is equity if dividends and redemption are at the issuer's discretion. For example (illustration), a subsidiary issues two classes of 10,000 each with a 6 percent dividend.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Class A: cumulative, never redeemable, dividend declared at RUPS discretion","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Class B: mandatorily redeemable at par after five years, dividend mandatory","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Classification","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financial liability","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issue","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Share capital, preference 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Redeemable preference shares 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual dividend of 600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Retained earnings 600 / Cr Dividends payable 600, only when declared","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Cash 600","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cumulative","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" means unpaid dividends carry forward and must be paid before ordinary dividends. Arrears are not dividends payable. No liability exists until the RUPS declares a dividend, so arrears on Class A are disclosed, not accrued. A share redeemable only at the issuer's option, with discretionary dividends, stays equity. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"participating","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" share also shares in profits beyond its fixed rate; a ","type":"text"},{"text":"convertible","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" one can be exchanged for ordinary shares.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Treasury shares","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shares a company buys back are ","type":"text"},{"text":"treasury shares","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The purchase returns capital to the sellers, so PSAK 232 deducts their cost from equity and records no gain or loss in profit or loss on their purchase, sale, issue or cancellation (para. 33).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesian law adds its own limits. A buyback must not reduce net assets below issued capital plus the mandatory reserve already set aside, and the nominal value of shares bought back must not exceed 10 percent of issued capital unless capital-market rules say otherwise (art. 37(1)). Treasury shares may be held for at most three years (art. 37(4)), need RUPS approval (art. 38), and carry no votes and no dividends (art. 40).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cost method, which most companies use, debits Treasury shares at cost and puts the result of a resale in equity. For example (illustration), a listed subsidiary buys back shares for 900, then resells half for 520 and the other half for 400.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase: Dr Treasury shares 900 / Cr Cash 900","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"First resale: Dr Cash 520 / Cr Treasury shares 450 / Cr Share premium, treasury shares 70","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Second resale: Dr Cash 400, Dr Share premium, treasury shares 50 / Cr Treasury shares 450; balance left = 70 − 50 = 20","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had the second resale come first, with no treasury-share premium to absorb it, the 50 would be debited to retained earnings. Profit is unaffected either way.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The net-asset test compares net assets in the company's latest statements approved by the RUPS with issued capital plus the mandatory reserve, and the law states issued capital in rupiah. For example (illustration), a company with issued capital of Rp100 billion and a mandatory reserve of Rp20 billion has net assets of Rp150 billion, of which Rp30 billion is the negative balance of a common-control difference.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Headroom = 150 − (100 + 20) = Rp30 billion; without the negative APIC it would be Rp60 billion","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For PHE the test cannot be run from the published statements. It applies to the company's own net assets, PHE publishes only consolidated figures, and its year-end rate is disclosed only as USD 0.06 per Rp1,000 (Note 3r). The consolidated figures show only the direction. In USD at historical rates, the negative APIC of 3,649,862 offsets most of the retained earnings of 4,352,620.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Common-control restructurings: PSAK 338","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The problem.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If each transfer of a subsidiary within a group were an acquisition under PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Business Combinations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3), the receiving company would remeasure everything at fair value and recognise goodwill, though nothing left the group and no outsider set a price. PSAK 103 excludes combinations of entities under common control from its scope, and IFRS has no standard for them, so Indonesia keeps its own standard, PSAK 338 (2012) ","type":"text"},{"text":"Business Combinations of Entities under Common Control","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 38, renumbered from 1 January 2024). PHE's 2024 statements apply this version; a 2025 revision, effective 2026, is described below.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The principle.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A transfer of a business within a group to reorganise it is not a change of ownership in economic substance, so it can create no profit or loss for the group or for any entity in it (para. 07). One example is a parent transferring a subsidiary to another subsidiary it owns (para. 08(b)). Buying out non-controlling shareholders is excluded, because ownership does change (para. 09). The receiving entity uses the ","type":"text"},{"text":"pooling-of-interests method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" at carrying amounts (para. 10), and:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"recognises the difference between the consideration and the carrying amount of the business in equity, presented in APIC (para. 11);","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"presents the current and comparative periods as if the combination had happened from the start of common control (para. 12);","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"expenses the combination's costs, except costs of issuing debt or equity, which follow PSAK 109 and PSAK 232 (para. 14).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The transferring entity books its own difference, between the consideration received and the carrying amount it gives up, in APIC too (para. 15). These APIC balances are never recycled to profit or reclassified to retained earnings, even if the business later leaves the group (Basis for Conclusions DK12–DK14). PSAK 38 (2004) had kept the difference in a separate account (SNTRES) and released it to profit when common control ended; its balances moved to APIC on 1 January 2013 (para. 20).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An example.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For example (illustration, sized to sit close to PHE's APIC of −3,649,862; PHE does not disclose its own combinations' figures), a parent transfers 100 percent of an upstream company to its subholding. The company's net assets have a carrying amount of 6,000,000; the fair value of its identifiable net assets is 8,500,000; an unaffiliated appraiser values the business at 9,650,000. The subholding pays by issuing new shares with a nominal value of 9,650,000, which art. 34 allows because the contribution is valued at 9,650,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition method (PSAK 103, if the parties were unrelated)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pooling of interests (PSAK 338)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net assets recognised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,500,000 at fair value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000,000 at carrying amount","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,650,000 − 8,500,000 = 1,150,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"None","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Difference","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"None","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,650,000 − 6,000,000 = 3,650,000, debit to APIC","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Prior periods","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not restated; results from acquisition date","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restated as if combined since common control began","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal (consolidated)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Net assets 8,500,000, Dr Goodwill 1,150,000 / Cr Share capital 9,650,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Net assets 6,000,000, Dr APIC 3,650,000 / Cr Share capital 9,650,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Company law values the contribution at fair value and lets the nominal value of shares issued for it go up to that amount, never above it, with any excess of value over nominal recorded as agio. PSAK 338 puts the asset side at book value. When the parties set the nominal near fair value, or pay cash above book value, the gap is a debit, a negative APIC. DSAK IAI answered the objection that this looks like disagio. The legal ban concerns only the initial payment of capital, and APIC holds more than agio, so a negative common-control difference may sit there (DK10).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent books the mirror image (Dr Investment in subholding 9,650,000 / Cr Investment in subsidiary 6,000,000 / Cr APIC 3,650,000), and on consolidation at the ultimate parent the two APIC entries cancel.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whose carrying amounts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The 2012 text, which rejected push-down accounting (DK19–DK22), points to the carrying amounts in the ","type":"text"},{"text":"transferred entity's","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" own statements, so goodwill or fair-value uplifts the parent recognised when it first bought the business stay behind. PHE's policy says \"the book value of the net assets of the acquiree\" (Note 3d), though IAI found practice divided (DK23A of the 2025 exposure draft). The 2025 revision (approved 29 October 2025, effective 1 January 2026, prospective) switches to the ","type":"text"},{"text":"transferring entity's","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" carrying amounts unless impracticable and allows impracticable restatement to be skipped (exposure draft paras. 10A–10C, 12A–12B).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restating comparatives.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian practice shows the equity of a business not yet legally owned in a separate column of the statement of changes in equity, closed against share capital and APIC when the transfer is completed (practice, not the standard's text). PHE's statement of changes in equity has such a column, \"Penggabungan Entitas/Merging Entities\", which is nil at every date shown (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16), consistent with no common-control combination in progress in 2023 or 2024. It matters for an upstream group because Note 1b lists PHE's subsidiaries largely as one company per working area (Daftar 5/4, PDF p. 21), so blocks change hands inside the Pertamina group by moving shares.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Other components of contributed capital","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"APIC can also hold contributions with no shares issued, such as the benefit of a below-market shareholder loan (see the Long-term bank loans essay). Other equity lines hold items that are neither profit nor OCI: changes in a parent's stake in a subsidiary without loss of control (PSAK 110, formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10), share-based payment reserves (PSAK 102), and the equity component of convertible bonds. PSAK 201 asks for the nature and purpose of each reserve and a reconciliation of every component.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Share capital across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and insurers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PP 8/2021, which implements the amended art. 32, keeps the higher minimums that sector rules set for certain businesses (art. 5); before Cipta Kerja the elucidation to art. 32(2) named banking, insurance and freight forwarding. Commercial banks had to hold core capital (paid-up capital plus reserves and retained earnings) of at least Rp3 trillion by the end of 2022 under POJK 12/POJK.03/2020. Insurers face a minimum equity under POJK 23/2023. For both, the regulator's capital measure is wider than legal capital, and a new licence also needs a minimum paid-up capital.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"State-owned enterprises.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The state funds a Persero by ","type":"text"},{"text":"penyertaan modal negara","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (state equity participation), in cash or in kind, and builds holdings by inbreng of state shares. Government Regulation 6/2018 added to the state's equity in Pertamina by contributing the state's 13,809,038,755 Series B shares in Perusahaan Gas Negara, with the value set by the Minister of Finance; the state kept control through the Series A Dwiwarna share, a single share with special rights.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Listed companies.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Capital-market law can override UU PT on buyback limits (art. 37(1)(b)) and on no-par shares (art. 49(3)). Listed companies raise equity through rights issues (","type":"text"},{"text":"hak memesan efek terlebih dahulu","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the pre-emptive right in tradeable form), whose costs follow section 3.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 31 and 32 and the statement of changes in equity","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's equity components and their 2024 movements (statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; statement of changes in equity, Daftar 3, PDF p. 16):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 Jan 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dividends","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reserve transfer","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity adjustment in subsidiaries","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Comprehensive income","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share capital","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13,631,466","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":