-- trade-payables-accounts-payable-and-supplier-finance
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).</p></li><li><p>Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.</p></li><li><p>A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of "trade payables" a bank has already paid.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade payables of 1,091,822 at 31 December 2024 against 883,688 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 7.1 percent of total liabilities and 17.7 percent of current liabilities (5.3 and 14.7 percent a year earlier), and 3.6 percent of total assets (2.9 percent). Beside it PHE reports other payables of 613,718 (481,963) and other current liabilities of 224 (324), which this page also covers.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to say when a payable exists, record it through its life in journals, judge whether a supplier finance arrangement has turned it into a borrowing, and read a payables note.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a liability is, and which liabilities are current</h2><p>A liability is a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events (<em>Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting</em> 2018, para. 4.26). For a payable the past event is the delivery of goods or the performance of a service. An undelivered purchase order for drilling chemicals is no liability yet.</p><p>A common working definition of a current liability is one settled within a year with a resulting cash outflow. The 2018 framework drops the probability language from the definition, so uncertainty about the outflow affects recognition and measurement, and PSAK 201 adds the operating cycle to the one-year test.</p><p><strong>Current or non-current.</strong> PSAK 201 <em>Presentation of Financial Statements</em> (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) classifies a liability as current if any of four tests holds: the entity expects to settle it in its normal operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months after the reporting date, or has no right at that date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (para. 69). Trade payables and accruals for operating costs are part of the working capital used in the operating cycle, so they are current even when due more than twelve months later (para. 70). PHE's policy uses the twelve-month and operating cycle tests (Note 3t, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). The 2024 amendments to PSAK 201 on covenants and the right to defer settlement bite mainly on loans; see the long-term bank loans essay.</p><p><strong>Payable, accrual or provision.</strong> PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) draws the line (para. 11). <em>Trade payables</em> are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. <em>Accruals</em> are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have not been invoiced or formally agreed. <em>Provisions</em> are liabilities of uncertain timing or amount. For example (illustration), three items at a PHE subsidiary on 31 December 2024:</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What is known</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Line</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Workover rig hire for November, invoiced 20 December, 1,800 due on 19 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount and date</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade payables</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Helicopter flying hours in December, logged at 240 but not yet invoiced</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount nearly certain, no invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrued expenses</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A contractor's claim of 3,000 for standby days, disputed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount and timing uncertain</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>provision or contingent liability</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The accrued expenses essay and the decommissioning provision essay own the second and third rows.</p><h2>2. Recognising and measuring accounts payable</h2><p>A payable arises from a time lag between receiving goods or services and paying for them. Recognition follows the transfer of control. For goods that depends on the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point the payable arises when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, under FOB destination when they arrive (see the inventories essay). For a service the trigger is performance.</p><p><strong>Initial measurement.</strong> A trade payable is a financial liability under PSAK 109 <em>Financial Instruments</em> (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) and is first measured at fair value. For a short-term payable with no stated interest, fair value is the invoice amount, because discounting 30 or 60 days of credit changes nothing material. PHE states its payables at their "notional amounts, which approximate their fair values" (Note 3, financial liabilities, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63) and reports that its trade payables are unsecured and non-interest bearing (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130). Afterwards they are carried at amortised cost, which for a non-interest-bearing payable is the amount still owed.</p><p><strong>Cut-off.</strong> In practice a payable is booked after a <em>three-way match</em> of purchase order, receiving report and invoice, and a late invoice means a missed payable. For example (illustration), take the 803,062 that PHE owes third parties at the end of 2024 (Note 19) as the population, and suppose two items were missed at year end.</p><blockquote><p>Casing worth 2,000 shipped FOB shipping point on 29 December and invoiced on 30 December, invoice posted on 6 January: inventories and trade payables both understated by 2,000; profit unchanged</p><p>Well-servicing work worth 500 performed and invoiced in December, invoice posted in January: production expenses and trade payables understated by 500; profit overstated by 500</p><p>Correcting entry, 31 December: Dr Inventories 2,000, Dr Production expenses 500 / Cr Trade payables 2,500</p></blockquote><p>The first error only shrinks the balance sheet. The second flatters profit, which is why auditors search for unrecorded liabilities by reading payments made after year end. Had the invoices not yet arrived at year end, the same entry would credit accrued expenses instead (see the accrued expenses essay).</p><h2>3. Purchase discounts and the cost of not taking them</h2><p>A cash discount for prompt payment turns every payable into a small financing decision. It can be recorded in two ways. The <em>gross method</em> books the invoice amount and recognises the discount only when taken. The <em>net method</em> books the discounted amount, treating it as the real price, so a discount missed is shown as a finance cost (the inventories essay works both through to the cost of casing). For example (illustration), a purchase of 10,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30: a 2 percent discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full amount by day 30.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 10,000 / Cr Trade payables 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 9,800 / Cr Trade payables 9,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid on day 10</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Inventories 200 / Cr Cash 9,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 9,800 / Cr Cash 9,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid on day 30</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 9,800, Dr Purchase discounts lost 200 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The net method shows what the gross method hides. Paying on day 30 instead of day 10 borrows 9,800 for 20 days at a cost of 200.</p><blockquote><p>Rate for 20 days = 200 / 9,800 = 2.04%</p><p>Annual rate, simple = 2.04% × 365 / 20 = 37.2%; compounded = (1 + 2.04%)^(365/20) − 1 = 44.6%</p></blockquote><p>Any entity that can borrow below 37 percent a year should take the discount.</p><h2>4. Notes payable</h2><p>A note payable is a written promise to pay a set sum on a set date, here arising from an overdue account payable. There are two kinds.</p><p><strong>Interest-bearing notes</strong> state an interest rate, and interest accrues with time. For example (illustration), on 31 October a foreign supplier of a PHE subsidiary (PHE owes foreign suppliers 189,687, Note 19) agrees to convert an overdue invoice of 10,000 into a six-month note at 8 percent a year.</p><blockquote><p>31 October: Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 10,000</p><p>31 December, two months of interest = 10,000 × 8% × 2/12 = 133.33: Dr Interest expense 133.33 / Cr Interest payable 133.33</p><p>30 April, maturity, interest for six months = 400.00: Dr Notes payable 10,000, Dr Interest payable 133.33, Dr Interest expense 266.67 / Cr Cash 10,400</p></blockquote><p><strong>Zero-interest-bearing notes</strong> state only the amount due at maturity, so the interest sits in the gap between that amount and the present value received. A note of 110,000 due in a year at a market rate of 10 percent is recorded at 100,000, and 10,000 of interest expense accrues over the year; the long-term bank loans essay works such notes through.</p><h2>5. Payables in a foreign currency</h2><p>A payable in a currency other than the entity's functional currency is a <em>monetary item</em>, a right or obligation to a fixed number of currency units. PSAK 221 <em>The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates</em> (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) records it at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), retranslates it at the closing rate at each reporting date (para. 23), and takes the exchange differences to profit or loss (para. 28). PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, which uses the rupiah (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). Rupiah trade payables were 518,011 at the end of 2024 (Note 19b). Those of the dollar entities are retranslated through profit or loss; Elnusa's are translated on consolidation, with the difference in other comprehensive income. Note 48 shows the same payables as Rp8,369,504 million (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), which implies a closing rate of 8,369,504 / 518.011 = Rp16,157 per US dollar.</p><p>For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary receives a rupiah invoice for Rp32,000 million on 1 December 2024 when the rate is Rp15,800, and pays it on 15 January 2025 at Rp16,300.</p><blockquote><p>1 December: 32,000 / 15,800 × 1,000 = 2,025.32: Dr Production expenses 2,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 2,025.32</p><p>31 December at 16,157: 32,000 / 16,157 × 1,000 = 1,980.57; gain = 2,025.32 − 1,980.57 = 44.75: Dr Trade payables 44.75 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 44.75</p><p>15 January at 16,300: cash = 1,963.19; gain = 1,980.57 − 1,963.19 = 17.38: Dr Trade payables 1,980.57 / Cr Cash 1,963.19 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 17.38</p></blockquote><p>The expense stays at 2,025.32, the rate on the day of the service. The weaker rupiah cut the dollar cost of settlement by 62.13, split between the two years in which the rate moved.</p><h2>6. Related-party payables and offsetting</h2><p><strong>Related parties.</strong> A payable to a parent, a fellow subsidiary or another government-related entity is measured like any other payable, but PSAK 224 <em>Related Party Disclosures</em> (formerly PSAK 7, IAS 24) requires the relationship, the transactions and the outstanding balances to be disclosed, because the terms may not be those an independent supplier would set. Entities controlled by the same government may use a reduced disclosure for balances with one another (paras. 25–26). PHE discloses its related-party trade payables by counterparty (Note 44e, Daftar 5/185, PDF p. 202).</p><p><strong>Offsetting.</strong> A payable and a receivable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right of set-off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42); the trade receivables essay works this through PHE's Pertamina balances. PHE settles those balances by offset but presents them gross, a receivable of 18,434 and a payable of 105,797 (Note 44c, Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201).</p><h2>7. Supplier finance arrangements</h2><p>In a <em>supplier finance arrangement</em>, also called supply chain finance, payables finance or reverse factoring, one or more finance providers pay what an entity owes its suppliers, and the entity pays the provider on or after the date the suppliers are paid (PSAK 207, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7, para. 44G). The supplier gets cash early, priced on the buyer's credit, and the buyer often gets longer to pay.</p><p>The IFRS Interpretations Committee's December 2020 agenda decision on reverse factoring answered three questions from existing standards.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Presentation.</strong> A liability stays in trade payables only if it is a liability to pay for goods or services, is invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)), and is part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.70). Extra security given to the bank, or terms that differ materially from those of other payables, point to a separate line or to borrowings.</p></li><li><p><strong>Derecognition.</strong> If the obligation to the supplier is extinguished, the payable is derecognised and a new liability to the bank is recognised (section 8).</p></li><li><p><strong>Cash flows.</strong> These follow the classification. A payable that stays in working capital is settled through operating cash flows. If it has become a borrowing, the bank's payment to the supplier is a non-cash transaction (PSAK 207.43), and the later payment to the bank is a financing outflow.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a supplier 10,000 due in 60 days. A bank pays the supplier 9,900 on day 10, with the supplier bearing the 1 percent discount.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case A: entity pays the bank 10,000 on day 60, no security</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case B: entity pays the bank 10,150 on day 150, guaranteed by the parent</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Nature of the liability</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the same trade payable, now owed to the bank</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>a 140-day financing with interest and security</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Day 10</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry, or Dr Trade payables / Cr Trade payables, supplier finance, 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Day 150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000, Dr Interest expense 150 / Cr Cash 10,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow statement</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>operating outflow 10,000 on day 60</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no operating outflow; financing outflow 10,000 and interest 150 by policy</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>In case B operating cash flow is 10,000 higher than if the entity had paid the supplier itself, with nothing in its operations changed, which is why analysts asked for disclosure.</p><p><strong>The 2024 disclosures.</strong> Amendments to PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 <em>Financial Instruments: Disclosures</em> (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7), effective for annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024, require the terms of the arrangements; at the start and end of the period, the liabilities in them, the lines they sit in, and how much the providers have already paid to suppliers; the range of due dates against comparable payables outside the arrangements; and non-cash changes such as exchange differences (PSAK 207.44H). PSAK 107 brings supplier finance into the liquidity risk disclosures. PHE adopted the amendments in 2024 and reports no significant impact on its financial reporting (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48). It does not say whether it has any such arrangements. Yet Note 44e lists PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia (8,454) and PT Bank Mandiri (4,897) among related-party trade creditors, and a bank in trade payables is what a supplier finance arrangement or a usance letter of credit would produce. The absence of a PSAK 207.44H disclosure suggests these balances are immaterial or of another kind.</p><h2>8. Derecognition</h2><p>A payable leaves the balance sheet in one of two ways under PSAK 109.</p><p><strong>Extinguishment</strong> (para. 3.3.1). The obligation is discharged by payment, cancelled by the creditor, or expires. Waiting is not enough. For example (illustration), a subsidiary carries 300 owed to a supplier that has not chased it for three years. Unless the debt has been legally released or has expired under the law of the contract, the 300 stays. A write-back on the strength of silence overstates profit and understates liabilities.</p><p><strong>Substantial modification</strong> (paras. 3.3.2–3.3.3). A substantial change of terms is accounted for as extinguishing the old liability and recognising a new one at fair value, with the difference in profit or loss. The quantitative test is whether the new cash flows, discounted at the original effective interest rate, differ by at least 10 percent (para. B3.3.6; the long-term bank loans essay teaches it on a loan).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a struggling contractor 10,000, overdue. They agree that the contractor will accept 8,500 payable in one year, without interest. The original payable carried no interest, so its effective rate is 0 percent, and the market rate for this subsidiary's one-year debt is 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Present value of new cash flows at the original rate = 8,500 / 1.00 = 8,500</p><p>Difference = (10,000 − 8,500) / 10,000 = 15% ≥ 10%: extinguishment</p><p>Fair value of the new liability = 8,500 / 1.08 = 7,870.37</p><p>Gain = 10,000 − 7,870.37 = 2,129.63</p><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 7,870.37 / Cr Gain on extinguishment of payables 2,129.63</p><p>Over the year: Dr Interest expense 629.63 / Cr Notes payable 629.63, bringing it to 8,500</p></blockquote><p>Had the contractor accepted 9,500 instead, the difference would be 5 percent. The payable would stay, adjusted to 9,500 with a modification gain of 500.</p><h2>9. Trade payables across industries</h2><p><strong>Retail.</strong> Supermarkets often sell goods before paying for them, so days payable exceed the days goods sit on shelves plus the days customers take to pay, and working capital is negative. For example (illustration), days in inventory 40, days sales outstanding 2, days payable outstanding 60.</p><blockquote><p>Cash conversion cycle = 40 + 2 − 60 = −18 days</p></blockquote><p>Suppliers finance the business. Goods taken on <em>consignment</em> create no payable until the retailer sells them, because the supplier keeps control, and <em>supplier rebates</em> reduce the cost of inventory (PSAK 202.11).</p><p><strong>Construction.</strong> Contractors keep a <em>retention</em>, commonly 5 to 10 percent of each subcontractor's certified progress, until the work passes inspection. The retention is owed, with timing tied to completion. For example (illustration), a subcontractor certificate of 2,000 with 5 percent retention.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Contract costs 2,000 / Cr Trade payables 1,900 / Cr Retentions payable 100</p></blockquote><p><strong>Banks and insurers.</strong> A bank's main liabilities are customer deposits, at amortised cost under PSAK 109, and an insurer's claims payable sits inside insurance contract liabilities under PSAK 117 (IFRS 17). Neither has a large trade payables line.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> A buyer that purchases an asset under <em>murabahah</em>, a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin with deferred payment, records a murabahah payable for the full agreed price, with the margin as deferred murabahah expense, shown as a deduction from the payable and amortised as the payable is settled (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102; STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), equipment costing 1,000 bought with a margin of 100 payable in one year.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Equipment 1,000, Dr Deferred murabahah expense 100 / Cr Murabahah payable 1,100</p></blockquote><p>The economics are those of the zero-interest note in section 4, with the cost of credit called a margin.</p><p><strong>Upstream oil and gas.</strong> Joint operations create their own payables between operator and partners (section 11).</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 19, 21 and 44</h2><p>PHE splits its payables by supplier and by currency (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130; Note 21, Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, related parties (Note 44e)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>288,760</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>312,718</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, local suppliers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>613,375</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>383,055</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, foreign suppliers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>189,687</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>187,915</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Trade payables</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,091,822</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>883,688</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Of which in rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>518,011</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>413,080</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other payables, related parties (Note 44f)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>155,090</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>168,204</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other payables from subsidiaries' oil and gas operations</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>429,695</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300,799</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Due to operators, cash call shortfalls</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>28,933</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,960</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Other payables</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>613,718</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>481,963</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Trade payables rose 23.6 percent from the end of 2023. Third parties account for more than all of it (up 40.6 percent, mostly local suppliers), while related-party payables fell 7.7 percent. Rupiah payables were 47.4 percent of the line.</p><p><strong>Days payable outstanding.</strong> DPO measures how long an entity takes to pay its suppliers.</p><blockquote><p>DPO = average trade payables / purchases on credit × 365</p></blockquote><p>PHE does not disclose purchases, but its direct-method cash flow statement shows cash paid to suppliers of 5,117,737 in 2024 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), which can stand in for them.</p><blockquote><p>Average trade payables = (1,091,822 + 883,688) / 2 = 987,755</p><p>DPO ≈ 987,755 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 70 days</p><p>At year end: 1,091,822 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 78 days (2023: 883,688 / 4,640,992 × 365 = 69 days)</p></blockquote><p>The proxy is rough. Trade payables include suppliers of drilling and other capital work, whose payments sit in investing cash flows (purchase of oil and gas properties, 3,933,616 in 2024 and 3,948,075 in 2023). Adding them to the denominator gives about 44 days at year end (2023: 38). Both measures rise, so the slower payment at the end of 2024 does not depend on the proxy.</p><p><strong>Related parties.</strong> Related-party trade payables were 1.88 percent of total liabilities, led by PT Pertamina (Persero) 105,797, PT Pertamina Patra Niaga 73,164 and PT Perusahaan Gas Negara 30,891; the list also includes PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia 8,454, PT Bank Mandiri 4,897 and the Ministry of Finance 3,665 (Note 44e). Note 44e says these payables arise from drilling, gas processing and other oil and gas services, but Note 44c labels the whole 105,797 owed to Pertamina as "payables in relation to payment of cash calls", Pertamina's funding of the group's operations, offset against what it owes for sales. A liability for cash advanced is not a liability for goods or services invoiced by a supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)). The balance barely moved over the year (103,376 a year earlier), Note 49d describes "funding arrangements with Pertamina", and the cash flow statement reports Pertamina funding within operating activities, nil in 2024 and an outflow of 4,633 in 2023 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). This page reads the 105,797 as closer to a borrowing; PHE does not explain the classification. The two banks are related to PHE through banking services (Note 44a, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199), a point section 7 takes up.</p><p><strong>Maturity.</strong> PHE's liquidity table puts all trade and other payables in the "less than one year" band (Note 49d, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250).</p><p><strong>Other current liabilities</strong> of 224 (324) carry no note reference.</p><h2>11. Upstream note: cash calls and joint interest billings</h2><p>PHE's subsidiaries operate some working areas and are non-operators in others, and both roles create payables. A partner in a joint operation recognises its share of jointly incurred liabilities (PSAK 111, formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). PHE's policy for unitised fields says that as operator it recognises a payable to non-operators when the <em>cash calls</em>, requests for funds in advance, it has received exceed their share of revenues, costs, capital spending and liabilities, and a receivable when they fall short (Note 3y, Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92). As non-operator, PHE classifies its proportional share of the operator's <em>joint interest billing</em> (JIB) reports, statements of actual spending, as other payables, 429,695 at the end of 2024, and owes operators 28,933 of cash calls not yet paid (Note 21). The same relationship runs the other way in advances: cash calls paid to operators ahead of spending are 76,196 (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). For example (illustration), a non-operating subsidiary with a 30 percent interest receives a JIB showing 5,000 of operating costs for December.</p><blockquote><p>Share = 5,000 × 30% = 1,500</p><p>Dr Production expenses 1,500 / Cr Other payables, JIB 1,500</p></blockquote><p>Note 21 gives the basis for putting them in other payables, that they come from "a proportional share of the Joint Interest Billing (JIB) report" sent by the operator.</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses">accrued-expenses</a>: services received but not yet invoiced, before they become payables.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: where uncertain obligations and contingencies are taught.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net">trade-receivables-net</a>: offsetting, worked through PHE's Pertamina balances.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/inventories-net">inventories-net</a>: the asset side of purchases on credit, and purchase discounts.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">advances-prepayments-current</a>: cash calls paid to operators in advance.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-payable</a>: VAT and withholding taxes on supplier invoices.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">long-term-bank-loans-non-current</a>: where a supplier finance liability that has become a borrowing would sit; zero-interest notes, the 10 percent test and the PSAK 201 covenant amendments.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That the 105,797 owed to Pertamina for cash calls is closer to a borrowing than to a trade payable is this page's reading of Notes 44c, 44e and 49d and the cash flow statement against PSAK 237.11(a). PHE does not explain the classification, the two notes describe the same balances differently, and Pertamina funding is reported in operating cash flows.</p></li><li><p>Note 19's English text says PHE's trade payables "were denominated in US Dollars", while the Indonesian text, the governing version, says "berdominasi" (mostly) US dollars, and the currency table shows 47.4 percent in rupiah. This page follows the Indonesian text and the table.</p></li><li><p>DPO rests on proxies, because PHE does not disclose credit purchases. Cash paid to suppliers alone gives about 70 days on average (year end 69 to 78); adding payments for oil and gas properties gives 38 to 44 at year end. The level depends on the denominator; the rise does not.</p></li><li><p>Why PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia and PT Bank Mandiri (13,351 together, 1.2 percent of trade payables) appear among trade creditors is not disclosed; supplier finance or usance letters of credit are this page's hypotheses. The Ministry of Finance's 3,665 may be rent for state-owned assets (Note 44a lists asset lease services), but that is inference.</p></li><li><p>The murabahah buyer entries under PSAK 402 and the PSAK 117 placement of insurers' claims payable are from knowledge and were not checked against the PSAK text.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b new standards (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3 financial liabilities and offsetting (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 3t (Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87); Note 3y (Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133); Note 44a, 44c, 44e, 44f (Daftar 5/182–5/186, PDF pp. 199–203); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49d (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p><em>Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting</em> (IASB 2018; Indonesian <em>Kerangka Konseptual Pelaporan Keuangan</em>), para. 4.26. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (from knowledge).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 207 <em>Laporan Arus Kas</em> (IAS 7) paras. 43, 44F–44H and the 2023 amendments <em>Supplier Finance Arrangements</em> (with PSAK 107 / IFRS 7, effective 1 January 2024). Content VERIFIED (web, Commission Regulation (EU) 2024/1317 adopting the IAS 7 and IFRS 7 amendments, via lawplayer.com). Indonesian effective date VERIFIED (web, PwC Indonesia, <em>Practical Guide to PSAK 2024</em>; PHE Note 3b).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision <em>Supply Chain Financing Arrangements—Reverse Factoring</em> (December 2020), including the presentation criteria (IAS 37.11(a), IAS 1.70), derecognition and cash flow analysis. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org compilation of agenda decisions vol. 4; Forvis Mazars, "Reverse factoring – Guidance on the applicable requirements under IFRS", May 2021).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 109 <em>Instrumen Keuangan</em> (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1–3.3.3 and B3.3.6 (10 percent test). B3.3.6 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers on fees in the 10 per cent test). Other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–70; PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 11; PSAK 221 (IAS 21) paras. 21, 23, 28 (number VERIFIED: PHE Note 3b lists the PSAK 221 amendment); PSAK 224 (IAS 24) paras. 25–26; PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11); PSAK 202 (IAS 2) para. 11; PSAK 117 (IFRS 17); PSAK 402 (murabahah). PSAK 201.70 and 237.11 content VERIFIED (web, via ref. 4); PSAK 232.42 wording matches PHE's policy (ref. 1). Numbers follow the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n; sharia standards 1xx → 4xx); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, ch. 13 <em>Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies</em>. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of \"trade payables\" a bank has already paid.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade payables of 1,091,822 at 31 December 2024 against 883,688 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 7.1 percent of total liabilities and 17.7 percent of current liabilities (5.3 and 14.7 percent a year earlier), and 3.6 percent of total assets (2.9 percent). Beside it PHE reports other payables of 613,718 (481,963) and other current liabilities of 224 (324), which this page also covers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to say when a payable exists, record it through its life in journals, judge whether a supplier finance arrangement has turned it into a borrowing, and read a payables note.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a liability is, and which liabilities are current","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A liability is a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events (","type":"text"},{"text":"Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" 2018, para. 4.26). For a payable the past event is the delivery of goods or the performance of a service. An undelivered purchase order for drilling chemicals is no liability yet.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A common working definition of a current liability is one settled within a year with a resulting cash outflow. The 2018 framework drops the probability language from the definition, so uncertainty about the outflow affects recognition and measurement, and PSAK 201 adds the operating cycle to the one-year test.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current or non-current.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Presentation of Financial Statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) classifies a liability as current if any of four tests holds: the entity expects to settle it in its normal operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months after the reporting date, or has no right at that date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (para. 69). Trade payables and accruals for operating costs are part of the working capital used in the operating cycle, so they are current even when due more than twelve months later (para. 70). PHE's policy uses the twelve-month and operating cycle tests (Note 3t, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). The 2024 amendments to PSAK 201 on covenants and the right to defer settlement bite mainly on loans; see the long-term bank loans essay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payable, accrual or provision.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) draws the line (para. 11). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accruals","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have not been invoiced or formally agreed. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities of uncertain timing or amount. For example (illustration), three items at a PHE subsidiary on 31 December 2024:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What is known","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Line","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Workover rig hire for November, invoiced 20 December, 1,800 due on 19 January","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount and date","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade payables","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Helicopter flying hours in December, logged at 240 but not yet invoiced","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount nearly certain, no invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued expenses","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A contractor's claim of 3,000 for standby days, disputed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount and timing uncertain","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"provision or contingent liability","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The accrued expenses essay and the decommissioning provision essay own the second and third rows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Recognising and measuring accounts payable","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable arises from a time lag between receiving goods or services and paying for them. Recognition follows the transfer of control. For goods that depends on the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point the payable arises when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, under FOB destination when they arrive (see the inventories essay). For a service the trigger is performance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial measurement.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A trade payable is a financial liability under PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) and is first measured at fair value. For a short-term payable with no stated interest, fair value is the invoice amount, because discounting 30 or 60 days of credit changes nothing material. PHE states its payables at their \"notional amounts, which approximate their fair values\" (Note 3, financial liabilities, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63) and reports that its trade payables are unsecured and non-interest bearing (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130). Afterwards they are carried at amortised cost, which for a non-interest-bearing payable is the amount still owed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cut-off.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In practice a payable is booked after a ","type":"text"},{"text":"three-way match","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" of purchase order, receiving report and invoice, and a late invoice means a missed payable. For example (illustration), take the 803,062 that PHE owes third parties at the end of 2024 (Note 19) as the population, and suppose two items were missed at year end.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Casing worth 2,000 shipped FOB shipping point on 29 December and invoiced on 30 December, invoice posted on 6 January: inventories and trade payables both understated by 2,000; profit unchanged","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Well-servicing work worth 500 performed and invoiced in December, invoice posted in January: production expenses and trade payables understated by 500; profit overstated by 500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Correcting entry, 31 December: Dr Inventories 2,000, Dr Production expenses 500 / Cr Trade payables 2,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The first error only shrinks the balance sheet. The second flatters profit, which is why auditors search for unrecorded liabilities by reading payments made after year end. Had the invoices not yet arrived at year end, the same entry would credit accrued expenses instead (see the accrued expenses essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Purchase discounts and the cost of not taking them","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A cash discount for prompt payment turns every payable into a small financing decision. It can be recorded in two ways. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"gross method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" books the invoice amount and recognises the discount only when taken. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"net method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" books the discounted amount, treating it as the real price, so a discount missed is shown as a finance cost (the inventories essay works both through to the cost of casing). For example (illustration), a purchase of 10,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30: a 2 percent discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full amount by day 30.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 10,000 / Cr Trade payables 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 9,800 / Cr Trade payables 9,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on day 10","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Inventories 200 / Cr Cash 9,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 9,800 / Cr Cash 9,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on day 30","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 9,800, Dr Purchase discounts lost 200 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The net method shows what the gross method hides. Paying on day 30 instead of day 10 borrows 9,800 for 20 days at a cost of 200.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rate for 20 days = 200 / 9,800 = 2.04%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual rate, simple = 2.04% × 365 / 20 = 37.2%; compounded = (1 + 2.04%)^(365/20) − 1 = 44.6%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Any entity that can borrow below 37 percent a year should take the discount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Notes payable","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A note payable is a written promise to pay a set sum on a set date, here arising from an overdue account payable. There are two kinds.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest-bearing notes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" state an interest rate, and interest accrues with time. For example (illustration), on 31 October a foreign supplier of a PHE subsidiary (PHE owes foreign suppliers 189,687, Note 19) agrees to convert an overdue invoice of 10,000 into a six-month note at 8 percent a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 October: Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 10,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December, two months of interest = 10,000 × 8% × 2/12 = 133.33: Dr Interest expense 133.33 / Cr Interest payable 133.33","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30 April, maturity, interest for six months = 400.00: Dr Notes payable 10,000, Dr Interest payable 133.33, Dr Interest expense 266.67 / Cr Cash 10,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Zero-interest-bearing notes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" state only the amount due at maturity, so the interest sits in the gap between that amount and the present value received. A note of 110,000 due in a year at a market rate of 10 percent is recorded at 100,000, and 10,000 of interest expense accrues over the year; the long-term bank loans essay works such notes through.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Payables in a foreign currency","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable in a currency other than the entity's functional currency is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"monetary item","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a right or obligation to a fixed number of currency units. PSAK 221 ","type":"text"},{"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) records it at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), retranslates it at the closing rate at each reporting date (para. 23), and takes the exchange differences to profit or loss (para. 28). PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, which uses the rupiah (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). Rupiah trade payables were 518,011 at the end of 2024 (Note 19b). Those of the dollar entities are retranslated through profit or loss; Elnusa's are translated on consolidation, with the difference in other comprehensive income. Note 48 shows the same payables as Rp8,369,504 million (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), which implies a closing rate of 8,369,504 / 518.011 = Rp16,157 per US dollar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary receives a rupiah invoice for Rp32,000 million on 1 December 2024 when the rate is Rp15,800, and pays it on 15 January 2025 at Rp16,300.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 December: 32,000 / 15,800 × 1,000 = 2,025.32: Dr Production expenses 2,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 2,025.32","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December at 16,157: 32,000 / 16,157 × 1,000 = 1,980.57; gain = 2,025.32 − 1,980.57 = 44.75: Dr Trade payables 44.75 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 44.75","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15 January at 16,300: cash = 1,963.19; gain = 1,980.57 − 1,963.19 = 17.38: Dr Trade payables 1,980.57 / Cr Cash 1,963.19 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 17.38","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The expense stays at 2,025.32, the rate on the day of the service. The weaker rupiah cut the dollar cost of settlement by 62.13, split between the two years in which the rate moved.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Related-party payables and offsetting","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Related parties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A payable to a parent, a fellow subsidiary or another government-related entity is measured like any other payable, but PSAK 224 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Related Party Disclosures","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 7, IAS 24) requires the relationship, the transactions and the outstanding balances to be disclosed, because the terms may not be those an independent supplier would set. Entities controlled by the same government may use a reduced disclosure for balances with one another (paras. 25–26). PHE discloses its related-party trade payables by counterparty (Note 44e, Daftar 5/185, PDF p. 202).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Offsetting.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A payable and a receivable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right of set-off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42); the trade receivables essay works this through PHE's Pertamina balances. PHE settles those balances by offset but presents them gross, a receivable of 18,434 and a payable of 105,797 (Note 44c, Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Supplier finance arrangements","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"supplier finance arrangement","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", also called supply chain finance, payables finance or reverse factoring, one or more finance providers pay what an entity owes its suppliers, and the entity pays the provider on or after the date the suppliers are paid (PSAK 207, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7, para. 44G). The supplier gets cash early, priced on the buyer's credit, and the buyer often gets longer to pay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IFRS Interpretations Committee's December 2020 agenda decision on reverse factoring answered three questions from existing standards.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Presentation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A liability stays in trade payables only if it is a liability to pay for goods or services, is invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)), and is part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.70). Extra security given to the bank, or terms that differ materially from those of other payables, point to a separate line or to borrowings.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Derecognition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If the obligation to the supplier is extinguished, the payable is derecognised and a new liability to the bank is recognised (section 8).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flows.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" These follow the classification. A payable that stays in working capital is settled through operating cash flows. If it has become a borrowing, the bank's payment to the supplier is a non-cash transaction (PSAK 207.43), and the later payment to the bank is a financing outflow.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a supplier 10,000 due in 60 days. A bank pays the supplier 9,900 on day 10, with the supplier bearing the 1 percent discount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case A: entity pays the bank 10,000 on day 60, no security","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case B: entity pays the bank 10,150 on day 150, guaranteed by the parent","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nature of the liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the same trade payable, now owed to the bank","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a 140-day financing with interest and security","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Day 10","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry, or Dr Trade payables / Cr Trade payables, supplier finance, 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Day 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000, Dr Interest expense 150 / Cr Cash 10,150","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"operating outflow 10,000 on day 60","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no operating outflow; financing outflow 10,000 and interest 150 by policy","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In case B operating cash flow is 10,000 higher than if the entity had paid the supplier itself, with nothing in its operations changed, which is why analysts asked for disclosure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 disclosures.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Amendments to PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments: Disclosures","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7), effective for annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024, require the terms of the arrangements; at the start and end of the period, the liabilities in them, the lines they sit in, and how much the providers have already paid to suppliers; the range of due dates against comparable payables outside the arrangements; and non-cash changes such as exchange differences (PSAK 207.44H). PSAK 107 brings supplier finance into the liquidity risk disclosures. PHE adopted the amendments in 2024 and reports no significant impact on its financial reporting (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48). It does not say whether it has any such arrangements. Yet Note 44e lists PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia (8,454) and PT Bank Mandiri (4,897) among related-party trade creditors, and a bank in trade payables is what a supplier finance arrangement or a usance letter of credit would produce. The absence of a PSAK 207.44H disclosure suggests these balances are immaterial or of another kind.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Derecognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable leaves the balance sheet in one of two ways under PSAK 109.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Extinguishment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" (para. 3.3.1). The obligation is discharged by payment, cancelled by the creditor, or expires. Waiting is not enough. For example (illustration), a subsidiary carries 300 owed to a supplier that has not chased it for three years. Unless the debt has been legally released or has expired under the law of the contract, the 300 stays. A write-back on the strength of silence overstates profit and understates liabilities.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Substantial modification","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" (paras. 3.3.2–3.3.3). A substantial change of terms is accounted for as extinguishing the old liability and recognising a new one at fair value, with the difference in profit or loss. The quantitative test is whether the new cash flows, discounted at the original effective interest rate, differ by at least 10 percent (para. B3.3.6; the long-term bank loans essay teaches it on a loan).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a struggling contractor 10,000, overdue. They agree that the contractor will accept 8,500 payable in one year, without interest. The original payable carried no interest, so its effective rate is 0 percent, and the market rate for this subsidiary's one-year debt is 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value of new cash flows at the original rate = 8,500 / 1.00 = 8,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Difference = (10,000 − 8,500) / 10,000 = 15% ≥ 10%: extinguishment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value of the new liability = 8,500 / 1.08 = 7,870.37","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain = 10,000 − 7,870.37 = 2,129.63","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 7,870.37 / Cr Gain on extinguishment of payables 2,129.63","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over the year: Dr Interest expense 629.63 / Cr Notes payable 629.63, bringing it to 8,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had the contractor accepted 9,500 instead, the difference would be 5 percent. The payable would stay, adjusted to 9,500 with a modification gain of 500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Trade payables across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Retail.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Supermarkets often sell goods before paying for them, so days payable exceed the days goods sit on shelves plus the days customers take to pay, and working capital is negative. For example (illustration), days in inventory 40, days sales outstanding 2, days payable outstanding 60.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash conversion cycle = 40 + 2 − 60 = −18 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Suppliers finance the business. Goods taken on ","type":"text"},{"text":"consignment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" create no payable until the retailer sells them, because the supplier keeps control, and ","type":"text"},{"text":"supplier rebates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" reduce the cost of inventory (PSAK 202.11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Contractors keep a ","type":"text"},{"text":"retention","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", commonly 5 to 10 percent of each subcontractor's certified progress, until the work passes inspection. The retention is owed, with timing tied to completion. For example (illustration), a subcontractor certificate of 2,000 with 5 percent retention.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract costs 2,000 / Cr Trade payables 1,900 / Cr Retentions payable 100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and insurers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A bank's main liabilities are customer deposits, at amortised cost under PSAK 109, and an insurer's claims payable sits inside insurance contract liabilities under PSAK 117 (IFRS 17). Neither has a large trade payables line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A buyer that purchases an asset under ","type":"text"},{"text":"murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin with deferred payment, records a murabahah payable for the full agreed price, with the margin as deferred murabahah expense, shown as a deduction from the payable and amortised as the payable is settled (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102; STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), equipment costing 1,000 bought with a margin of 100 payable in one year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Equipment 1,000, Dr Deferred murabahah expense 100 / Cr Murabahah payable 1,100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The economics are those of the zero-interest note in section 4, with the cost of credit called a margin.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream oil and gas.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Joint operations create their own payables between operator and partners (section 11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 19, 21 and 44","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE splits its payables by supplier and by currency (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130; Note 21, Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, related parties (Note 44e)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"288,760","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"312,718","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, local suppliers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"613,375","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"383,055","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, foreign suppliers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"189,687","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"187,915","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,091,822","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"883,688","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Of which in rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"518,011","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"413,080","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables, related parties (Note 44f)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"155,090","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"168,204","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables from subsidiaries' oil and gas operations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"429,695","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300,799","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Due to operators, cash call shortfalls","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"28,933","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,960","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"613,718","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"481,963","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables rose 23.6 percent from the end of 2023. Third parties account for more than all of it (up 40.6 percent, mostly local suppliers), while related-party payables fell 7.7 percent. Rupiah payables were 47.4 percent of the line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Days payable outstanding.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" DPO measures how long an entity takes to pay its suppliers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO = average trade payables / purchases on credit × 365","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE does not disclose purchases, but its direct-method cash flow statement shows cash paid to suppliers of 5,117,737 in 2024 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), which can stand in for them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Average trade payables = (1,091,822 + 883,688) / 2 = 987,755","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO ≈ 987,755 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 70 days","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At year end: 1,091,822 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 78 days (2023: 883,688 / 4,640,992 × 365 = 69 days)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The proxy is rough. Trade payables include suppliers of drilling and other capital work, whose payments sit in investing cash flows (purchase of oil and gas properties, 3,933,616 in 2024 and 3,948,075 in 2023). Adding them to the denominator gives about 44 days at year end (2023: 38). Both measures rise, so the slower payment at the end of 2024 does not depend on the proxy.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Related parties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Related-party trade payables were 1.88 percent of total liabilities, led by PT Pertamina (Persero) 105,797, PT Pertamina Patra Niaga 73,164 and PT Perusahaan Gas Negara 30,891; the list also includes PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia 8,454, PT Bank Mandiri 4,897 and the Ministry of Finance 3,665 (Note 44e). Note 44e says these payables arise from drilling, gas processing and other oil and gas services, but Note 44c labels the whole 105,797 owed to Pertamina as \"payables in relation to payment of cash calls\", Pertamina's funding of the group's operations, offset against what it owes for sales. A liability for cash advanced is not a liability for goods or services invoiced by a supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)). The balance barely moved over the year (103,376 a year earlier), Note 49d describes \"funding arrangements with Pertamina\", and the cash flow statement reports Pertamina funding within operating activities, nil in 2024 and an outflow of 4,633 in 2023 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). This page reads the 105,797 as closer to a borrowing; PHE does not explain the classification. The two banks are related to PHE through banking services (Note 44a, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199), a point section 7 takes up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Maturity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE's liquidity table puts all trade and other payables in the \"less than one year\" band (Note 49d, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other current liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" of 224 (324) carry no note reference.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: cash calls and joint interest billings","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's subsidiaries operate some working areas and are non-operators in others, and both roles create payables. A partner in a joint operation recognises its share of jointly incurred liabilities (PSAK 111, formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). PHE's policy for unitised fields says that as operator it recognises a payable to non-operators when the ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash calls","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", requests for funds in advance, it has received exceed their share of revenues, costs, capital spending and liabilities, and a receivable when they fall short (Note 3y, Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92). As non-operator, PHE classifies its proportional share of the operator's ","type":"text"},{"text":"joint interest billing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (JIB) reports, statements of actual spending, as other payables, 429,695 at the end of 2024, and owes operators 28,933 of cash calls not yet paid (Note 21). The same relationship runs the other way in advances: cash calls paid to operators ahead of spending are 76,196 (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). For example (illustration), a non-operating subsidiary with a 30 percent interest receives a JIB showing 5,000 of operating costs for December.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share = 5,000 × 30% = 1,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,500 / Cr Other payables, JIB 1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 21 gives the basis for putting them in other payables, that they come from \"a proportional share of the Joint Interest Billing (JIB) report\" sent by the operator.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": services received but not yet invoiced, before they become payables.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where uncertain obligations and contingencies are taught.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-receivables-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": offsetting, worked through PHE's Pertamina balances.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"inventories-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the asset side of purchases on credit, and purchase discounts.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"advances-prepayments-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": cash calls paid to operators in advance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-taxes-payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": VAT and withholding taxes on supplier invoices.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-bank-loans-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where a supplier finance liability that has become a borrowing would sit; zero-interest notes, the 10 percent test and the PSAK 201 covenant amendments.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the 105,797 owed to Pertamina for cash calls is closer to a borrowing than to a trade payable is this page's reading of Notes 44c, 44e and 49d and the cash flow statement against PSAK 237.11(a). PHE does not explain the classification, the two notes describe the same balances differently, and Pertamina funding is reported in operating cash flows.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 19's English text says PHE's trade payables \"were denominated in US Dollars\", while the Indonesian text, the governing version, says \"berdominasi\" (mostly) US dollars, and the currency table shows 47.4 percent in rupiah. This page follows the Indonesian text and the table.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO rests on proxies, because PHE does not disclose credit purchases. Cash paid to suppliers alone gives about 70 days on average (year end 69 to 78); adding payments for oil and gas properties gives 38 to 44 at year end. The level depends on the denominator; the rise does not.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Why PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia and PT Bank Mandiri (13,351 together, 1.2 percent of trade payables) appear among trade creditors is not disclosed; supplier finance or usance letters of credit are this page's hypotheses. The Ministry of Finance's 3,665 may be rent for state-owned assets (Note 44a lists asset lease services), but that is inference.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The murabahah buyer entries under PSAK 402 and the PSAK 117 placement of insurers' claims payable are from knowledge and were not checked against the PSAK text.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b new standards (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3 financial liabilities and offsetting (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 3t (Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87); Note 3y (Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133); Note 44a, 44c, 44e, 44f (Daftar 5/182–5/186, PDF pp. 199–203); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49d (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IASB 2018; Indonesian ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kerangka Konseptual Pelaporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), para. 4.26. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (from knowledge).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Arus Kas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 7) paras. 43, 44F–44H and the 2023 amendments ","type":"text"},{"text":"Supplier Finance Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (with PSAK 107 / IFRS 7, effective 1 January 2024). Content VERIFIED (web, Commission Regulation (EU) 2024/1317 adopting the IAS 7 and IFRS 7 amendments, via lawplayer.com). Indonesian effective date VERIFIED (web, PwC Indonesia, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Practical Guide to PSAK 2024","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; PHE Note 3b).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision ","type":"text"},{"text":"Supply Chain Financing Arrangements—Reverse Factoring","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (December 2020), including the presentation criteria (IAS 37.11(a), IAS 1.70), derecognition and cash flow analysis. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org compilation of agenda decisions vol. 4; Forvis Mazars, \"Reverse factoring – Guidance on the applicable requirements under IFRS\", May 2021).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1–3.3.3 and B3.3.6 (10 percent test). B3.3.6 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers on fees in the 10 per cent test). Other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–70; PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 11; PSAK 221 (IAS 21) paras. 21, 23, 28 (number VERIFIED: PHE Note 3b lists the PSAK 221 amendment); PSAK 224 (IAS 24) paras. 25–26; PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11); PSAK 202 (IAS 2) para. 11; PSAK 117 (IFRS 17); PSAK 402 (murabahah). PSAK 201.70 and 237.11 content VERIFIED (web, via ref. 4); PSAK 232.42 wording matches PHE's policy (ref. 1). Numbers follow the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n; sharia standards 1xx → 4xx); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, ch. 13 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of accounts payable from the definition of a liability to supplier finance arrangements, worked through PHE's payables to suppliers, partners and the Pertamina group and set beside the industries where payables behave differently","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).","Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.","A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of \"trade payables\" a bank has already paid."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'a1561fc6-765d-4fc0-84b1-a5da1a890033' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
